\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\b \uc2 EMT ABDUCTION MYSTERY CIRCUMSTANCES STILL UNCLEAR FOLLOWING DISAPPEARANCE\b0\par\par\uc2 598 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TORNADOES, TREVI\u209\'00\'D1O, VILLARREAL, FLORES\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, STORM, TRACK\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION, TITLE 42, HANCOCK, NEUHAUS, LEESER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIGRANT CRISIS OVERWHELMS U.S. CITIES\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS, TRUMP, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESANTIS SEEKS IOWA SUPPORT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DEBT CEILING, NEGOTIATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN: DEBT CEILING TALKS "NOT THERE YET"\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, POPE FRANCIS, KLITSCHKO, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIAL: RUSSIA TARGETED PATRIOT SYSTEM\par \par \uc2 JORDAN, ABDUCTION, STENNETT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EMT ABDUCTION MYSTERY\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Lachelle Jordan now speaking out. The Cleveland EMT missing for days until she turned up barefoot and in torn clothing 3 miles from where she was last seen. Here's ABC's Mona Kosar Abdi.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CIRCUMSTANCES STILL UNCLEAR FOLLOWING DISAPPEARANCE\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the family of a missing Ohio woman found alive are searching for answers, but calling it a miracle that she came home.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH JORDAN (FATHER OF LACHELLE JORDAN)\par \par \uc2 Lachelle has had some trauma. She's had some injuries and she's been hospitalized and she's getting medical treatment.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lachelle Jordan, a Cleveland EMT, had been missing since Saturday, last seen by her family after she went outside to grab something from her car. Her family and authorities searching for days. Then, on Friday, the 30-year-old reappeared, limping into a convenience store barefoot with tattered clothes. The chilling moment caught on surveillance video. Jordan using the clerk phone to call 911.\par \par \uc2 DISPATCHER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You will never believe this. We just got a call from Lachelle Jordan. She said she got away from the people that kidnapped her. They tried to burn her alive.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The mother of two was rushed to the hospital. The alleged kidnapping taking place before she was set to testify in a rape and kidnapping case involving 65-year-old Michael Stennett, who was reportedly a former co-worker. Jordan also reported that Stennett violated a restraining order several times, the most recent just two days before she went missing.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Whit, police say that Stennett has not been connected to Jordan's disappearance. Last May, he was indicted on two counts of rape and one count of abduction. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mona Kosar Abdi, our thanks to you tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH CAROLINA GOV. VETOES BILL\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The battle over abortion rights in North Carolina is intensifying.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abortion rights supporters rallying in the state's capitol as democratic governor Roy cooper vetoed a bill that would have banned nearly all abortions after 12 weeks. Republicans who now have a veto-proof majority in the legislature hoping to override that veto and pass the measure into law.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSTHUMOUS DEGREES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TO UNIVERSITY OF IDAHO VICTIMS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And tonight, emotional moments at the University of Idaho commencement ceremonies.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The four students stabbed to death off campus last fall were honored. The two seniors, Madison Mogen and Kelly Goncalves awarded bachelor's degrees. Madison's family coming forward to receive the honor. Certificates were also awarded to Xana Kernodle and Ethan Chapin who were juniors.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's still much more ahead on "World News Tonight" this Saturday.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The dramatic rescue as officers try to save a horse struggling off shore. And half a million people are told to evacuate ahead of a monster storm.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020230516ej5d00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW BREAST CANCER SCREENING RECOMMENDATION TASK FORCE: WOMEN SHOULD START MAMMOGRAMS AT 40\b0\par\par\uc2 789 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP LIABLE FOR ABUSE & DEFAMATION\par \par \uc2 CARROLL, JURY, TRUMP, LIABLE, SEXUAL ABUSE, DEFAMATION\par \par \uc2 CARROLL, KAPLAN, VERDICT, TRUMP, TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 SANTOS, CHARGES, DOJ, CAMPAIGN, FINANCES\par \par \uc2 DEBT CEILING, BIDEN, REPUBLICANS, DEFAULT, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION, RESTRICTIONS, BORDER, SURGE, STORRUD\par \par \uc2 INFLATION, ECONOMISTS, PLATEAU, REPORT\par \par \uc2 NBA PLAYOFFS, \uc2 NUGGETS\uc2 , SUNS, \uc2 76ERS\uc2 , CELTICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 RICHINS, ARREST, MURDER, GRIEF, BOOK\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR, SHOT, GUN, ZWERNER, ELLENSON, TEACHER\par \par \uc2 BUDDY HOLLY, HAYES, BISHER, BEST IN SHOW\par \par \uc2 KROGER, FOOD, CEO, MCMULLEN, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FACING HIGH FOOD PRICES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KROGER CEO ON GROCERY TRENDS HEADING INTO SUMMER\par \par \uc2 BIRTH CONTROL, \uc2 FDA\uc2 , PILL, PRESCRIPTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OVER-THE-COUNTER BIRTH CONTROL PILL?\par \par \uc2 SCREENING, BREAST CANCER, MAMMOGRAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW BREAST CANCER SCREENING RECOMMENDATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TASK FORCE: WOMEN SHOULD START MAMMOGRAMS AT 40\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn to our "GMA" cover story and the major news about breast cancer screenings, now recommending that women should start getting screened at age 40. And our chief medical correspondent, Dr. Jen Ashton, here to join us and walk us through this. So, I sent so many text messages to friends and family yesterday about this. Can you break this down for us, Jen?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, well, first some historical context here. Back in 2009, 2010, the same group, the US Preventive Service Task Force, did the opposite. They raised the screening age from women at average risk starting at 40 to 50. It was met with uproar. Now, they have about 14 years of data. They've made adjustments in their recommendations, and here's what they're suggesting now, and these are draft recommendations. For average risk cisgender woman, for basic screening with mammograms to start at 40 and then basically every other year. Now, just to keep the controversy going, the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  and \uc2 ACOG\uc2  have their own slightly different recommendations as to when and how often, with the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  recommending that an average risk woman start at age 40 and then every year until at least age 55, \uc2 ACOG\uc2 , my organization, recommending average risk women start at 40 and then every year or every other year at least until age 75. So, when, how often and even what imaging modality still very up in the air.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you're a doctor. You talk every day to patients about these critical decisions about their health. Why the change here?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think in medicine, science and public health, we always have to stay open-minded to the incorporation and analysis of new data. And what they've found is that women in their 40s are getting more cancers, and Black women in particular are at a 40 percent higher risk of dying after a breast cancer diagnosis. So, they're shifting those recommendations. But again, a lot of people back in 2009, 2010 kind of forecasted this and said if you change the screening age, then this is probably what we're going to see.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, you know, there's always very polarized opinions. What we still do not know and what a lot of experts, including myself, are asking for clarification on is recommendations specifically for women in different groups, so women with dense breasts, which is 50 percent of women, Black women, women over the age of 75, who are kind of really left in a gray zone, and then women with a first degree family history of breast cancer. There are no specific recommendations for these groups, and in 2023, in the age of individualized medicine and targeted personalized medicine, there really should be.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're the doctor writing the scripts. So, what's the advice to your patients?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, listen, according to the CDC, one in four women don't get screened at all, so we want women to be screened for cancer because, in general in the world of oncology, any time you catch a cancer early, you increase the risk of survival, I mean the odds for survival. So, anyone getting a breast cancer screening should be prepared for further testing, but get screened. That's the most important thing.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I've done it. I'm so glad that I have, and I'm so happy to have you here, Dr. Ashton, to break it down.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you have to talk to your health care provider.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Dr. Ashton.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You bet.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230607ej5a00039\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PANEL: START MAMMOGRAMS AT 40 EXPERTS SAY 19% MORE LIVES COULD BE SAVED\b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, CARROLL, VERDICT, KAPLAN, TACOPINA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, VERDICT, SEXUAL ASSAULT\par \par \uc2 SANTOS, CHARGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REP. GEORGE SANTOS CHARGED\par \par \uc2 DEBT CEILING, ECONOMY, MCCARTHY, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEBT CEILING DEADLOCK\par \par \uc2 MALL, SHOOTING, GARCIA, SIBLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MALL MASSACRE INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION, CRISIS, TITLE 42, STORRUD, HOBBS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING AT THE BORDER\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, SCREENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PANEL: START MAMMOGRAMS AT 40\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to turn now to major health news tonight involving women and breast cancer screenings. A federal task force now recommending that women start getting mammograms at age 40. So, as always, we turn right to our chief medical editor Dr. Jen Ashton who's with us tonight. Obviously, Jen, you've read min to this. What were the concerns that led to this earlier age?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think, according to this group, it was based on new data. So, let's get right to what they're recommending now in these draft recommendations.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXPERTS SAY 19% MORE LIVES COULD BE SAVED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER SCREENING\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For the average risk woman to start mammogram and screening at age 40, and then every other year. They previously had recommended age 50. Why are they doing this? Diagnoses are rising in women in this age group. And in particular, they're seeing higher death rates in black women, 40% more likely to die after a breast cancer diagnosis. And according to the CDC, about one in four women are not up-to-date on their screenings. So, David, as for controversy, many experts asking for more clarification for certain groups, women with dense breast, women over the age of 75, and black women and we still don't have that yet.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, questions to be answered in the weeks ahead. Jen, Ashton tonight. Thank you, Jen.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020230511ej5900008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMY KLOBUCHAR LIVE IN TIMES SQUARE SENATOR TALKS ABOUT NEW MEMOIR OF HER LAST FOUR YEARS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,187 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MALL MASS SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, MASSACRE, VICTIMS, WITNESSES, TERRORISM\par \par \uc2 SUV, CRASH, MIGRANTS, INJURED, SANDOVAL, MANDON, BUS STOP\par \par \uc2 BRODER, TITLE 42, MIGRANTS, SURGE, PANDEMIC\par \par \uc2 POLL, BIDEN, AGE, TRUMP, DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 SUBWAY, NEELY, CHOKEHOLD, PENNY, PROTESTS, MILLS\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , \uc2 76ERS\uc2 , CELTICS, SUNS, \uc2 NUGGETS\uc2 , LAKERS, WARRIORS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORM REPORTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WOODS, HERMAN, LAWSUIT, HARASSMENT, NDA, CAMERON-BANKS\par \par \uc2 TURBOTAX, SETTLEMENT, DECEPTIVE, CHECKS, \uc2 INTUIT\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, CHARLES, CAMILLA, WILLIAM, CONCERT\par \par \uc2 FOXX, HOSPITALIZED, SMITH, STABLE, "PEOPLE", RECOVERY\par \par \uc2 CRUISE, CORONATION, PLANE, \uc2 MTV\uc2  AWARDS, FORMULA ONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DROUGHT MONITOR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAVY RAIN FORECAST\par \par \uc2 MALL, SHOOTING, GARCIA, BARNWELL\par \par \uc2 KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HORSE DEATHS PUT RACING UNDER SCRUTINY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING QUESTIONS AFTER SEVEN DIE IN RUN UP TO KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 MENTAL HEALTH, HEART DISEASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENTAL WELLNESS & HEART AND BRAIN HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW IT CAN HAVE A POSITIVE OR NEGATIVE IMPACT\par \par \uc2 GATES, ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE, NUCLEAR ENERGY\par \par \uc2 KEYS, SWIFT, "SPEAK NOW"\par \par \uc2 MENTAL HEALTH, CHEAH, PATEL, AANHPI\par \par \uc2 PETERSON, WURDEMAN, TROTTER, ENGEL, TATE, SMIT, RAMICH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEACHER APPRECIATION WEEK\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY, "WALKING WITH SAM"\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR, "THE JOY OF POLITICS"\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now with Senator Amy Klobuchar. The senior senator from Minnesota has a new book. "The Joy of Politics" is a memoir of the last four years. And to give you the flavor of the last four years, we're going to read the subtitle, "Surviving Cancer, Campaign, Pandemic and an Insurrection, and Life's Other Curve Balls." What didn't you deal with in the last four years?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMY KLOBUCHAR LIVE IN TIMES SQUARE\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 Completely. But you know what I think, George, America dealt with all of this. And so, I thought this is a good time to write a book not just lamenting the setbacks. And we've all had them. People have lost their jobs. They went through the pandemic. They lost loved ones. There's divisiveness, political divisiveness, violence. And yet, America keeps going forward. And so, my story is about how I went forward through the breast cancer and my husband almost dying of COVID.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) From COVID.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 And just looking for those moments of joy and rejoicing in the comebacks.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I have to say, it seems pretty hard to find the joy in politics these days. How do you do it?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 I do it by looking at the good. And really, we're only as good as the people who show up to push us to do good. And for me, that means things like helping a veteran get his benefits or passing a bill finally that I've worked on for years to bring down the cost of prescription drugs or making sure that we make clear that we're going to talk to people we don't always agree with for the betterment of this country. That that's actually courage in politics. And even the breast cancer, when I went public with my diagnosis actually on this show with Robin, I had tons of people write me, "I'm finally going into get an exam." And the outcome in some cases where they found out they had breast cancer and they followed up with me and said they got treatment and they're 100% good.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, there are always ways you can make a difference in someone's life.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Including that teacher we appreciated today. That's close to your hometown?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 Exactly. I grew up in Plymouth. I went to the schools there. I could just see Mr. Peterson in my mind, custodian turned teacher. That's doing good.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course, you ran for president before. We have this new poll out, ABC News and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS YOUR VICE YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some pretty tough messages there for President Biden. He's got the lowest approval rating of his presidency, losing to both \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Lots of concerns about his age. What do you say to your constituents that say, I kind of like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but I just don't know if I can go for him again?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 President Biden has gotten us through the pandemic. He's gotten us to a much better place in our economy and has ruled with steady hand. No one wants to go back to that chaos of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . People don't want to have that divide. And so, what I say to them is, this is a good man. He deserves your support.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally, we've had tragedies again over the weekend, 199th mass shooting in the country. How frustrating is it for you to sit in the Senate and know that nothing can be done?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 We did make a start, and you know that with the Gun Safety Bill. We worked really hard and finally took on the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , including a number of Republicans. To me that's the beginning. But when I think of the fact that 18-year-olds are buying assault weapons off the internet. By the way, that's what happened in Uvalde, Texas. That's what happened in Buffalo. People gunned down in a grocery store and school, little kids, it's time to do more. And to me that means banning the purchase of assault weapons. That means doing more when people are clearly mentally ill. They shouldn't be able to go out and get a gun.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 And what we saw was the beginning, but there must be more work. We just can't allow our babies to be killed like that. A little girl in a mall in Texas just gunned down by some guy that gets out of his car and starts shooting. That's not okay in America. We believe in freedom. And there's a lot of law-abiding gun owners in my state. We love to hunt in my state. It's a big thing. But you have to make sure who has those guns.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amen to that. Amy Klobuchar, thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (AUTHOR "THE JOY OF POLITICS")\par \par \uc2 Thanks, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "The Joy of Politics" is available tomorrow. And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "Good Morning America" is sponsored by\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's quite a start to the week.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230606ej580000r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WOMEN & HEART HEALTH STUDY: WOMEN HAVE HIGHER RISK OF HOSPITALIZATION\b0\par\par\uc2 747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DUST STORM, TRAVEL, DEVORE, STARRICK, THOMAS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORMS ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS WIND TAKES AIM AT SOUTHEAST, SPRING SNOW THREAT FOR NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 DEBT CEILING, BIDEN, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEBT LIMIT SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TREASURY SECRETARY WARS U.S. COULD DEFAULT BY JUNE 1ST\par \par \uc2 WRITERS GUILD, PROTEST, PATTEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLLYWOOD WRITERS GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNION & MEDIA COMPANIES FAIL TO AGREE ON NEW CONTRACT TERMS\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, OROPESA, SHOOTING, CRAWFORD, TORRES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT MANHUNT FOR SUSPECTED MASS KILLER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACCUSED GUNMAN WAS DEPORTED FROM U.S. AT LEAST FOUR TIMES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, TRIAL, CARROLL, SEXUAL ASSAULT, DEFAMATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CROSS-EXAMINATION CONCLUDES IN TRUMP CIVIL TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER PRESIDENT'S ASSAULT ACCUSER WRAPS UP THREE DAY S OF TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 BEHAVIOR, JIMENEZ, AIRPLANE, PASSENGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLIGHT FIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PASSENGER RESTRAINED AFTER APPEARING TO PUNCH AIRLINE EMPLOYEE\par \par \uc2 NBA PLAYOFFS, HARDEN, CURRY, JAMES, STANLEY CUP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 76ERS\uc2  TOP CELTICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JAMES HARDEN SCORES 45 TO LEAD PHILLY TO VICTORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 SUICIDE, BULLYING, REID, LIEBERMAN\par \par \uc2 REID, BULLYING\par \par \uc2 BEES, RESCUE, LOSCH, KEARNEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOTHER RESCUES CHILDREN FROM BEES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUTS KIDS IN CAR AS SHE'S STUNG 75 TIMES\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, KING CHARLES, PRINCESS CHARLOTTE, PRINCESS ANNE\par \par \uc2 SUPER BOWL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 DUST STORM, ILLINOIS, ACCIDENT, FATALITIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY DUST STORM PILEUP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST SIX DEAD, DOZENS INJURED IN CRASHES\par \par \uc2 STORMS, FORECAST, SNOW, WIND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORMS ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS WIND TAKES AIM AT SOUTHEAST\par \par \uc2 WOMEN, HEALTH, HEART, STUDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN & HEART HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY: WOMEN HAVE HIGHER RISK OF HOSPITALIZATION\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now we have two health headlines, a new study on women and heart health and the new report about loneliness. Dr. Jennifer Ashton is here with more, and let's start with women and heart.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Robin, we've known in gender-specific medicine that same disease, different outcomes, that women fare worse. What we didn't know is how they fared in the year following an acute heart attack. Take a look at what this study found. Women had a 1.5 times greater risk of being hospitalized compared to men in the year following their heart attack, and they had more than a two-time greater risk of death in that first year after a heart attack. So again, another glaring example, same disease, same condition, different outcome.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And signs and symptoms different?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE SIGNS & SYMPTOMS OF A HEART ATTACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN CAN EXPERIENCE DIFFERENT SYMPTOMS FROM MEN\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, well, so this is interesting. A lot of the symptoms are similar. So, let's go over the similar symptoms of a heart attack in men versus women, that chest pain or discomfort in the middle of the chest, shortness of breath or pain that radiates to the jaw, neck, back or arm. Those pretty classically same for men and women. What's different in the signs for a women is that they're, they tend to be more vague. They can masquerade as flu-like symptoms, nausea is more common, and then the less classic symptoms that we talked about before just are a little less prominent. So they're more subtle, oftentimes missed by the woman but also oftentimes missed by doctors.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALL TO ACTION TO FIGHT "EPIDEMIC OF LONELINESS"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SURGEON GENERAL: ISOLATION POSES RISKS AS DEADLY AS SMOKING\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, now let's get to that advisory that was just put out by the surgeon general about loneliness and the consequences of that.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Robin, think of loneliness now on par with (inaudible) or smoking. It has been associated with increased risk of diabetes, stroke, heart disease, dementia, death, you name it and the surgeon general really sounding the alarm on this. We, just because you can't see it as readily doesn't mean it's not there. So, that social engagement across all age groups, incredibly important. And we're going to be talking to the surgeon general on "GMA3" today about this initiative.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The surgeon general will be joining you on "GMA3."\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, he will.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you so much. Thanks for being with us here. Michael?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You bet.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230522ej5200065\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UNDERSTANDING MENOPAUSE WHAT ARE THE TYPICAL SIGNS THAT MAY BE STARTING MENOPAUSE?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,142 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST REPUBLIC BANK SOLD\par \par \uc2 FIRST REPUBLIC, \uc2 JP MORGAN\uc2 , \uc2 FDIC\uc2 , CHIAVRERINI\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADO, WIND, DAMAGE, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 STORMS, RAIN, FLOODING, SNOW\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SHOOTING, VICTIMS, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , SMITH, GUZMAN\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATIONS, CONVOY, HUMANITARIAN, CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, MERCENARY, AMMUNITION, UKRAINE, AIR STRIKES\par \par \uc2 FRANCE, PROTESTS, STRIKE, MACRON\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , WARRIORS, CURRY, \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , \uc2 76ERS\uc2 , CELTICS, PLAYOFFS, NHL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MISSISSIPPI RIVER FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SORENSON, CONVICTED, ABDUCTION, SOCIAL MEDIA, MARTINEZ, DRESOW\par \par \uc2 MORTGAGE, RULES, BORROWERS, FEES, CREDIT SCORE\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, COST, GUESTS, PRINCE HARRY\par \par \uc2 JENNY CRAIG, BANKRUPTCY, LAYOFFS, DIABETES, WEIGHT LOSS, ARONNE\par \par \uc2 NFL DRAFT, COWBOYS, VAUGHN, FATHER, SON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEMPERATURE SWING\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, JPMORGAN, BANKING CRISIS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESTRUCTIVE TORNADOES STRIKE VIRGINIA & FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOZENS OF HOMES DAMAGED AS SEVERE STORMS WREAK HAVOC\par \par \uc2 MELANOMA, BOWE, SKIN CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAISING AWARENESS OF MELANOMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT YOU NEED TO KNOW ABOUT THE DEADLY SKIN CANCER\par \par \uc2 FIRST REPUBLIC BANK\uc2 , \uc2 JPMORGAN CHASE\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, "HOT AND BOTHERED", DUNN, COX, OBAMA\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNDERSTANDING MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT ARE THE TYPICAL SIGNS THAT MAY BE STARTING MENOPAUSE?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now we're going to talk to Dr. Jennifer Ashton about this. Yes, we're going to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just like the author said, it's so important. This is not one size fits all. It's different for women, all of us.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No. That's right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But are there typical signs that you may be going through it?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's go through, you know, the standard GYN teaching, if you will, or facts about menopause.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Number one, average age of menopause in this country is 51. It can start as early around the age of 45 or even earlier. Perimenopause can start ten years before menopause actually begins. And our definition is one year without a menstrual period. In terms of the signs and symptoms literally, Robin, head-to-toe. We heard some of them in the piece, obviously, bleeding patterns can change, hot flushes, mood swings, trouble sleeping, skin changes, hair changes, weight changes.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is so pervasive and so distinctive woman to woman...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \u8230\'20\'26that we tend to say to a woman 40 and up, if you are experiencing something new, something different, think hormonal, think perimenopause or menopause first. That's how diverse and how broad the signs and symptoms can be.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Think of that first.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, because it's so broad. We heard from the author in Eva's piece some suggestions when you're having these symptoms, especially when it comes to hot flashes.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What are your recommendations?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO DEAL WITH THE SYMPTOMS OF HOT FLASHES?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, look, I think the first thing we have to remember is that hormone replacement therapy is and should be an option on the table for the vast majority of women.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALING WITH HOT FLASHES\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, women should talk to their gynecologist about that. There are numerous nonhormonal prescription medications that work very effectively to deal with hot flashes. And then we heard in the piece wellness behaviors, exercise, avoiding alcohol, caffeine, spicy foods which, for some reason, can trigger the onset of a hot flash. But, Robin, I want to be crystal clear. Hot flashes get all the attention.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we've talked about how many other signs and symptoms there can be. But just imagine having to go about your day-to-day life, whether it's in the home or workplace or socially and becoming completely drenched, red, hot, wet, sweaty? That's not consistent or compatible with, you know, years and years considering the average menopause symptoms can last seven years.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Unfortunately, I didn't have to imagine it. I did. I did.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You say imagine.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But many of us, myself included. Hormone replacement therapy, you brought that up.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, you know, there are some people who are a little leery of that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TALKING OPENLY ABOUT MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO HANDLE TRANSITION\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. So, first of all, you know, if you go back to 2002 and WHI data, which is what we refer to in gynecology as the shot heard around the world, when data was boiled down to a misleading headline, hormones bad, hormones dangerous. Now, there are oncologists who actually, in evaluating the data both past and present really find that most data suggests and shows conclusively that estrogen does not increase the risk of breast cancer. When we talk about other conditions that affect women, heart disease, osteoporosis, dementia, cause more deaths than breast cancer, and there are some real benefits to hormones. So, women have to discuss this with their gynecologists.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, and what if someone's, a woman who's watching right now, saying, I'm just not prepared for this?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think what's exciting now, women are living to 80s, 90s. Vibrant women in their 50s are talking about this, normalizing it. It's not about medicalizing a, quote/unquote, "natural phenomenon." Talk about the attention we give pregnancy, puberty. This deserves the same amount of attention in the workplace, socially, medically, societally, commercially, just keep going down the list. So, we can't talk about it enough. And by the way, men have to be involved in this conversation also.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you're going to have a conversation continuing about this on "GMA3."\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, "GMA3" the entire month, we're going to be talking about things dealing with menopause that you haven't heard before on all those levels. And that should be really good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Ali Wentworth is joining you tonight?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She sure is.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Thanks so much. Appreciate that, Jen.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You bet.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230522ej510003g\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LIVING THROUGH MENOPAUSE AUTHOR JANCEE DUNN ON WRITING THE BOOK SHE WISHED SHE'D HAD\b0\par\par\uc2 816 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST REPUBLIC BANK SOLD\par \par \uc2 FIRST REPUBLIC, \uc2 JP MORGAN\uc2 , \uc2 FDIC\uc2 , CHIAVRERINI\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADO, WIND, DAMAGE, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 STORMS, RAIN, FLOODING, SNOW\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SHOOTING, VICTIMS, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , SMITH, GUZMAN\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATIONS, CONVOY, HUMANITARIAN, CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, MERCENARY, AMMUNITION, UKRAINE, AIR STRIKES\par \par \uc2 FRANCE, PROTESTS, STRIKE, MACRON\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , WARRIORS, CURRY, \uc2 KNICKS\uc2 , \uc2 76ERS\uc2 , CELTICS, PLAYOFFS, NHL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MISSISSIPPI RIVER FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SORENSON, CONVICTED, ABDUCTION, SOCIAL MEDIA, MARTINEZ, DRESOW\par \par \uc2 MORTGAGE, RULES, BORROWERS, FEES, CREDIT SCORE\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, COST, GUESTS, PRINCE HARRY\par \par \uc2 JENNY CRAIG, BANKRUPTCY, LAYOFFS, DIABETES, WEIGHT LOSS, ARONNE\par \par \uc2 NFL DRAFT, COWBOYS, VAUGHN, FATHER, SON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEMPERATURE SWING\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, JPMORGAN, BANKING CRISIS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESTRUCTIVE TORNADOES STRIKE VIRGINIA & FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOZENS OF HOMES DAMAGED AS SEVERE STORMS WREAK HAVOC\par \par \uc2 MELANOMA, BOWE, SKIN CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAISING AWARENESS OF MELANOMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT YOU NEED TO KNOW ABOUT THE DEADLY SKIN CANCER\par \par \uc2 FIRST REPUBLIC BANK\uc2 , \uc2 JPMORGAN CHASE\par \par \uc2 MENOPAUSE, "HOT AND BOTHERED", DUNN, COX, OBAMA\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to many women pulling back the curtain on their journey with menopause, a topic that hasn't always been openly discussed. There's a new book taking on menopause myths, it's called "Hot and Bothered." Eva Pilgrim spoke with the author.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIVING THROUGH MENOPAUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHOR JANCEE DUNN ON WRITING THE BOOK SHE WISHED SHE'D HAD\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SERIES, "THE GOLDEN GIRLS"\par \par \uc2 RUE MCCLANAHAN (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It's menopause. Well, I wish I could die. Because as far as I'm concerned, this is the end of my life.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While it may have seemed that way once upon a time, in 2023, it's not your mother's menopause anymore. Countless celebrities sharing their personal experience of living through the transition, including Courteney Cox.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM INSTAGRAM VIDEO\par \par \uc2 COURTENEY COX (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Did your life change once a month because of your period?\par \par \uc2 COURTENEY COX (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Did your life completely change because of menopause?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  opening up on her podcast.\par \par \uc2 FORMER FIRST LADY \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It was like somebody put a furnace in my core and turned it on high.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Drew Barrymore on her show.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE (HOST "THE DREW BARRYMORE SHOW")\par \par \uc2 For the first time, I think I'm having my first hot flash. Whoa.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For Jancee Dunn, author of the new book "Hot and Bothered: What No One Tells You About Menopause and How to Feel Like Yourself Again," the first signs of menopause started when she turned 45.\par \par \uc2 JANCEE DUNN (AUTHOR "HOT AND BOTHERED")\par \par \uc2 When perimenopause did come for me, I had no idea that's what it was. I wasn't in that space at all.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dunn went to the library to see what books she could find on the life change.\par \par \uc2 JANCEE DUNN (AUTHOR "HOT AND BOTHERED")\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy books for days. One book on menopause. It was like from like 1992, dry, dusty, sad.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, she decided to talk to experts in the field and write the book she wished she'd had.\par \par \uc2 JANCEE DUNN (AUTHOR "HOT AND BOTHERED")\par \par \uc2 I really wanted to write a book that, you know, there was humor in it to not make it so dire.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Offering simple advice on how she handles some of the common symptoms like hot flashes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SIMPLE ADVICE ON HANDLING SOME OF THE MOST COMMON SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 JANCEE DUNN (AUTHOR "HOT AND BOTHERED")\par \par \uc2 I breathe in, count of five, and you hold it for eight counts, then breathe out. And there's something about giving yourself control.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MENOPAUSE SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Other symptoms to look out for, brain fog, mood changes, irregular periods, thinning hair and dry skin.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you go through menopause, your skin changes.\par \par \uc2 JANCEE DUNN (AUTHOR "HOT AND BOTHERED")\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How?\par \par \uc2 JANCEE DUNN (AUTHOR "HOT AND BOTHERED")\par \par \uc2 Okay. As estrogen leaves your body, it's probably going to get dryer and more sensitive and your go-to products might not work anymore. So, you want something that gets rid of that dry outer layer. Gentle retinol products can help.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What would you say to those women who, they're in the midst of it and struggling with it?\par \par \uc2 JANCEE DUNN (AUTHOR "HOT AND BOTHERED")\par \par \uc2 Normalize this transition by talking as much as possible to everyone you know about it. The more we talk about it, the more normal it is, the less scary it is.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For "Good Morning America," Eva Pilgrim, ABC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Eva. And "Hot and bothered" book comes out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230522ej510003f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,112 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABCTHISWEEK\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, 2024, CAMPAIGN, VOTERS, COONS, HARRIS, DEBT CEILING\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, MUHAMMAD, VILLAR, SYMES, RENNIX, KENYATTA, SIMS, GAMBLE, DAVIS, HELLER\par \par \uc2 BREAKDOWN, 2024, GOP, PRIMARY, TRUMP, DESANTIS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 SCALISE, BILL, VETERANS, BIDEN, MCCARTHY, 2024\par \par \uc2 WHCD, WOOD, BIDEN, 2024, VIDEO, DESANTIS, \uc2 DISNEY\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM SATURDAY\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 You say I'm ancient. I say I'm wise. They say I'm over the hill. Don Lemon would say that's a man in his prime.\par \par \uc2 ROY WOOD JUNIOR (COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 We should be inspired by the events in France. They rioted 'cause they didn't want to work 'til 64. Meanwhile, in America, we have an 80-year-old man begging us for four more years of work.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden and comedian Roy Wood Jr. joking about the President's age last night at the White House Correspondents' Dinner in Washington. Let's bring in the Roundtable to talk about all of this, former DNC Chair Donna Brazile, former Republican Congressman \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2 , our "Politico" senior political columnist Jonathan Martin, NPR's White House correspondent Asma Khalid. And that's who we were honoring last night, you White House correspondents.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But Donna, I wanna start with you. You said in February you didn't think President Biden should announce for a second term yet. Surprise, he did it. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN KICKS OFF 2024 CAMPAIGN WITH SOFT LAUNCH\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, first of all, he's, he's fired up and ready to go. I wasn't surprised that April 25th was the date. The video announcement, of course. More to come. But look, this is a president who's in a hurry. He has a long view of history, to invest in America's future, to ensure that the job that he started two years ago will be completed. And I do believe that this president is going to run as if he's ten points behind, understanding that the American people would like to make, would like to see him deliver, not just on the economy, but on those basic freedoms and the, and democracy that he promised a couple years ago.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and well, that's, that's what he concentrated on. He really didn't talk about his accomplishments in that video. He talked about January 6th. He talked about democracy. Is this the way to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if Trump, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's the nominee?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAKING STOCK OF THE 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 WILL HURD (REP) (FORMER TEXAS CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is operating as if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is gonna be the nominee, and his future success depends on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  being the nominee. When you have seven out of ten Americans doesn't wanna see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to run for reelection and six out of ten Americans not wanting to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  run as well, this is a, a potential rematch in 2024 that nobody wants to see. And unfortunately, whether we like it or not, age is going to be a factor in this campaign, 'cause guess what, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's no spring chicken either. And so, this is something that's, that both candidates are gonna have to deal with through their primaries and, and, and President Biden in the general election.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S AGE AT FOREFRONT OF VOTER CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Asma, I suppose you would agree about, about all of you, I mean, age is clearly...\par \par \uc2 ASMA KHALID (NPR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 It is.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...an issue in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 ASMA KHALID (NPR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And you hear that from voters.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cannot ignore it. You heard those, you heard those especially young voters talking about that. But, but, the rollout was, was pretty low key.\par \par \uc2 ASMA KHALID (NPR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 It was. It was. It was a video and then later in the day the President addressed, kind of, the legislative accomplishments that he's achieved. And I kind of see that from the White House's standpoint, you really hear them focusing on two visions. One is this broad idea of freedom and protecting freedom, whether that means, you know, abortion rights with the situation in Dobbs and the Supreme Court, and the other factor of this then is promoting legislative accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 538 POLLING AVG OVER HALF OF AMERICANS DISAPPROVE OF BIDEN\par \par \uc2 ASMA KHALID (NPR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 I mean, it is very difficult for this White House to ignore the President's approval ratings are still under water. They have been under water for more than a year ever since the withdrawal from Afghanistan. It would be, under any normal circumstances, very difficult for a president to have those approval ratings and win reelection, but this is not normal times.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, but, but Jonathan...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...he had those same approval ratings before the midterms.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But this is different.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Well, and precisely because of what happened in the midterms, the Biden folks had the same strategy for his reelect, which is make the focus on them, not us. It's hard to recall in modern political history a sitting president who was so eager to turn the attention away from him and his office toward the opposition. But that is the story, repeatedly, for this president and his administration. And I get the politics, okay, because for them it makes more sense to point to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than it is to talk about them because his numbers are terrible, in fact. So, I get the politics of it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 But it's remarkable that he puts out a video, gives a speech that nobody really pays attention to later that day. And that's the entirety of his reelection for a sitting president? But that's what we're in here. It's gonna be a Rose Garden campaign, to put it mildly. And the idea of sort of him engaging in the hurly-burly of the campaign is just never gonna happen. He's gonna be low-key, avoid the press and try to keep the focus on Trump and the GOP, which, since 2019 when he entered the race for the last time, has been the entirety of his strategy.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 And I think their thought, Martha, is if it ain't broke, don't fix it. But those numbers, though, are problematic under the hood. There's a lot of folks in his own party that don't want him to run again. And if the nominee is not Trump, then I think that calculation starts to change a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Donna, let, let me ask you about that. And you say, "Oh yeah, he's gonna be out, he's gonna be out there campaigning." The end of my piece, the young...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...voter, African-American voter who said, "Get me out of bed, Joe. Do something to make me want to go to the polls."\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Set an alarm.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. What, what does he do about that? I mean, as much as, you know, you hear Chris Coons talking and you're gonna hear all the surrogates saying this, people have been watching him and they, they are not sure.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, first of all, let me just quote the Chair of the Republican Party, something I rarely do. And she said, you know, focusing on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's age is not gonna be a winning issue. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just received a, a very good report from his doctors about his overall health. So, that's important. But this is a president who is totally focused on investing in America's future. Whether it's investing in our energy sector, - making sure that we can compete globally, this is a president who is totally focused on ensuring that every child gets a head start and a healthy start in life.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I'm not worried of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's ability to go out and campaign. I am worried about the Democrats, of course, being energized because that's a problem we always face. Let me just, another Republican, Will, you must, you're gonna like me. I - I'm giving Republicans some love. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in 1983, he waited very late. His approval ratings was even lower than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and he went on to whip us. And I say "whip" 'cause I worked on that season.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He whipped us, beat us, and every, all but, you know, Minnesota and the District of Columbia, so don't tell me about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's age. Don't tell me about his approval rating. Tell me what he's gonna do the help the American people lower cost like he's doing with prescription medication, making sure that America has jobs. That's what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will do.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Joe, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was older than his first day as president than \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was on his last day as president. He's not gonna escape the age issue. And it's gonna spill over to the focus on his vice president. And that announcement video was so striking because it, it featured the VP so much.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because she's in it more than a dozen times.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, at least.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that, that was very, that was very striking.\par \par \uc2 WILL HURD (REP) (FORMER TEXAS CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 It was but it...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's - it's almost like what Nikki Haley said, she didn't know whether he'd survive, so...\par \par \uc2 WILL HURD (REP) (FORMER TEXAS CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 Look, there's an opportunity for Republicans in 2024 if we have the right candidates, but we can't continue the mistakes that we made in 2016 and 2018, in 2020, and in 2022. And, and we need to put, put someone forward that has a, a vision for the future that shows how we can deal with inflation, how we make sure our allies around the world like us, how our enemies are gonna fear us, how do we deal with technology and make sure our kids are ready for jobs that don't exist today.\par \par \uc2 WILL HURD (REP) (FORMER TEXAS CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 When somebody puts forward that message, we have an opportunity in 2024, but too many people and Republican voters know this, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has too much baggage that is gonna prevent us from winning in, in this next upcoming election, like we've lost the House in - '18, the Senate in - the White House in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I - I wanna, I wanna talk about the GOP field. But, but, Asma, just, just finally on this point and, and the Biden run, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has not been front and center. And now, they're, it seems, pushing her front and center and, and giving her...\par \par \uc2 ASMA KHALID (NPR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 I, I will say she has been front and center, though, on one key issue that Democrats are trying to run on, and that is on abortion rights. You know, that is an issue that the White House has been eager to put her front and center on.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ASMA KHALID (NPR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And when I talk to, you know, inside the White House and outside in the Democratic Party, she seems to have a sense of credibility on that issue, and I would say, a sense legitimacy, perhaps even more so than the President has, right? She's more comfortable speaking on that issue.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMA KHALID (NPR WHITE HOUSE ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And I, I would say that no matter, you know, you talk about Trump is older. Biden is also older. I actually think there's an assumption that policy never matters in election cycle. That is probably it is always true until we came to abortion. I have not seen an issue in any recent election cycle that has galvanized the public in this way. And I think that Republicans will face - an insurmountable challenge if they cannot figure out how to deal in an effective way with that issue with where the public is.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICANS GRAPPLE WITH ABORTION DEBATE AHEAD OF 2024\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will, you want to go on that one?\par \par \uc2 WILL HURD (REP) (FORMER TEXAS CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 Look...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, the Republicans don't want to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 WILL HURD (REP) (FORMER TEXAS CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 Sure, - there's, I think policy does matter. And I think that's what people see. When, why do we have only 23 percent of the country voting in primaries? Because the rest of the country is like, we don't care what these, what these jokers are doing. And so, I think when you, when you put together these things that people actually care about, and guess what, I don't care if you're in a, a ruby red town or a deep blue city, you care about putting food on your table, a roof over our head and taking care of the, your loved ones.\par \par \uc2 WILL HURD (REP) (FORMER TEXAS CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 And, and if we talk about those issues, we have an opportunity. But if you're doings things like, you know, fighting businesses and, and trying to curb economic advancement and using the power of the government to stop businesses from operating, like, you're gonna have a big problem.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I think we know who you're talking about. Ron, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Jon, did you wanna jump in?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can, can I say something about this vice president?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah, please. Let's, let's Donna go.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She's been a very strong governing partner with this president. That's why she was included in the video. And she's not a one-issue person. She's someone who is, really has spent a lot of time on maternal healthcare, on climate. She spent a lot of time traveling across this country talking about voting rights and going across the globe talking about investing in America's future. So, she's gonna be a strong governing partner. And I think she's gonna be a true asset to this candidate.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Her numbers are terrible. And that's why the Biden folks know they have to build her up, because they know that the attacks coming are not gonna be just about him. It's gonna be a vote \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is actually a vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And that's the urgency that the Biden folks now, after three, two and a half years, are finally trying to help her, build her up some.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gen Z, 61 percent of Generation Z...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love that generation. They gave her strong approval ratings. You know what? She may not have been elected president.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Instead, she's vice president. And again, her job is to be a governing partner with this president.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jonathan, I want...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I wanna, I wanna turn to the GOP and - \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...and I want you to pick up there.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As, as Rick Klein noted, ABC's parent company \uc2 Disney\uc2  legally fired back after a year.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Florida Governor openly feuding with \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DISNEY SUES DESANTIS, ALLEGES GOV RETALIATED AGAINST COMPANY\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is, is that a, a good strategy there?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Look, I think his larger challenge, I, I think he's gonna run. He's putting together, I think, a pretty impressive campaign structure around him. I think his larger challenge is twofold. One is how to engage with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if to engage at all. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does total war, as they used to say in the military, and I think the Governor of Florida has never encountered that kind of a campaign assault. That's number one.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN (POLITICO POLITICS BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Number two is, I think perhaps his larger challenge, how to engage with Trump, it's "How do I put together a coalition of folks in my party who are sort of, Conservatives, kind of like me, but also bring along the people who don't like Trump but also don't like him that much." That's the challenge, Martha. How does he get kind of, the, the post-Trump party, the Conservatives and the kind of bushy, pre-Trump party together? Those folks don't want Trump, the latter crowd, but they're uneasy about him. And that's the real pirouette. You put together those two wings of the party, neither of which are excited about Trump, but one of which has doubts about DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks very much. It's a long road to the White House. We're gonna be doing this for a long time. It'll be a great fun. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Rachel Scott follows the money and the promises made to Black farmers after decades of discrimination. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020230503ej4u00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UTAH JUDGE WEIGHS CHALLENGE TO A BAN ON ABORTION CLINICS NORTH DAKOTA BANS ALL ABORTIONS, BANS FAIL IN NEBRASKA & SOUTH CAROLINA\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THUNDERSTORMS, TORNADOES AND FLOODING: DANGEROUS WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, SHOOTING, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 THREAT, WEATHER, SEVERE, FLOODING, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, MISSISSIPPI RIVER, EVACUATIONS, SHIPPING, GAS\par \par \uc2 HEAT, CALIFORNIA, SNOW, YOSEMITE\par \par \uc2 ARMY, AVIATORS, GROUNDED, COLLISIONS, MULROY\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATIONS, AMERICANS, PATEL\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, COUNTEROFFENSIVE, MISSILES\par \par \uc2 FEDERAL RESERVE, \uc2 FDIC\uc2 , \uc2 SILICON VALLEY BANK\uc2 , COLLAPSE\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, CHARLES, KING, ROYALS\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , DRAFT, LEVIS, TITANS\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS, BOOKS, BAN, FLORIDA, PICOULT, DESANTIS, CALDWELL-STONE\par \par \uc2 ROCK, ATTACKS, FATAL, ARREST, CHARGES\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, APPRENTICESHIP, GIULLANO, GOLDILLA, DOLAN, LAKHANI\par \par \uc2 BLUESKY, TWITTER, SCHIFFER, DORSEY, MUSK\par \par \uc2 LAKERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 STORMS, TORNADOES, TRACK\par \par \uc2 STORM, EVACUATIONS, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 BANKING CRISIS, ECONOMY, REPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL RESERVE & FDIC RELEASE REPORTS ON BANK FAILURE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIALS SAY COLLAPSE OF SIGNATURE BANK WAS DUE TO "POOR MANAGEMENT"\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BANS, ELECTION, ALITO\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're going to turn now to the battle over abortion rights intensifying in several traditionally red states ahead of the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UTAH JUDGE WEIGHS CHALLENGE TO A BAN ON ABORTION CLINICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH DAKOTA BANS ALL ABORTIONS, BANS FAIL IN NEBRASKA & SOUTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ABC's Zohreen Shah has more on that. Zohreen, good morning again.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Gio. Right now, many women in Utah are holding their breath. A judge there is delaying a ruling on whether to implement a ban on abortions. So, what does that mean? It means if you're in Utah, you can still get an abortion right now up to 18 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Meanwhile, there was another blow for the anti-abortion movement, and this time it was in Nebraska. There was an effort to ban abortion after the sixth week of pregnancy, but that fell one vote short of breaking the filibuster. Abortion there now remains banned after 20 weeks. And nearly a year after the Supreme Court's Dodd's decision was leaked, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  is speaking out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE SAMUEL ALITO SPEAKS ON ABORTION DRAFT LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAYS HE HAS A "PRETTY GOOD IDEA" WHO DID IT, "THIS MADE US TARGETS OF ASSASSINATION"\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He is telling "The Wall Street Journal" that that the leak made the justices targets of assassination. So, he doesn't believe any of the judges leaked it, but he said he thinks he knows who leaked the draft. And he says he is certain about what their motive was. Alito says he believes it was part of an effort to prevent the Dodd's draft from becoming a decision of the court.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As for the current national abortion debate, the Supreme Court has preserved access to the abortion drug Mifepristone, for now at least. Guys?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Zohreen Shah, thanks so much. We appreciate it. Coming up here in "GMA Morning Menu," are Americans losing confidence in the value of a college degree, and how the higher education landscape is now changing.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 3 MINUTES, COLLEGE OR BUST?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 6 MINUTES, MISSISSIPPI STRONG\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also ahead, Mississippi strong. The ongoing tornado recovery in Rolling Fork and how you can still help.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 11 MINUTES, MOTHER'S DAY MUST-HAVES\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, hitting the motherlode. "Deals & Steals" perfect for the month figures in your life. Stick around. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The motherlode.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230605ej4t00068\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FED ADMITS FAILURE REPORT: REGULATORS SHARE BLAME IN SILICON VALLEY BANK COLLAPSE\b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRACK, WEATHER, STORMS, ALVARADO, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 HELICOPTER, CRASH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY CHOPPER CRASHES\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, ZELENSKYY, MISSILE ATTACKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN MISSILE STRIKES\par \par \uc2 BANK COLLAPSE, SILICON VALLEY, BARR, HOGAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FED ADMITS FAILURE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here at home, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is blaming itself for a failure of oversight that contributed to the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  last month. And tonight, another bank, \uc2 First Republic\uc2 , is teetering on the edge. Here's ABC's Elizabeth Schulze.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPORT: REGULATORS SHARE BLAME IN SILICON VALLEY BANK COLLAPSE\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, seven weeks after the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  sparked fears of a financial crisis, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is partly blaming itself for the second-biggest bank failure in US history. An internal review finding regulators failed to take forceful-enough action to deal with growing risks as \uc2 SVB\uc2  exploded in size and its uninsured deposits bloomed. The Feds also blaming executive, saying they failed to address 31 out any warnings by the time the bank collapse.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BARR (VICE CHAIRMAN US FEDERAL RESERVE)\par \par \uc2 This is a textbook case of bank mismanagement.\par \par \uc2 STAFF MEMBER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You can take all your money.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Last month, it all came crashing down in just 48 hours as customers raced to pull their money out.\par \par \uc2 BANK CUSTOMER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 All our money is in the bank. I don't know how we're going to do our payroll.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just two days later, \uc2 Signature Bank\uc2  folding. The \uc2 FDIC\uc2  today saying their staff and the bank managers should have collapsed prevented that collapse, too. But tonight, there is fear that the banking crisis may not be over. \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2  is still struggling to stay afloat even after a $30 billion rescue for major banks.\par \par \uc2 ART HOGAN (CHIEF MARKET STRATEGIST B RILEY WEALTH)\par \par \uc2 I wouldn't be surprised if, come Monday, we have some news that \uc2 First Republic\uc2  is owned by and we won't be able to fill in the blank on Monday.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  now wants to tighten regulations on midsize banks that were rollback under the Trump administration, adding it can do that without approval from Congress. Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  says those changes could help make the bank system safer and more resilient. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Elizabeth Schulze, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROPOSED BANS FAIL\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're following news on abortion tonight. With surprising votes in two conservative states. Legislatures in Nebraska South Carolina with Republican majorities failing to pass strict new bands.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nebraska's Heartbeat Act to ban most abortions after six weeks falling one vote shy of defeating opponents' filibuster. While in South Carolina, the Senate rejected a bill that would have banned abortion from conception, except for rape, incest and other narrow exceptions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUSTICE ALITO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "PRETTY GOOD IDEA"\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , who wrote the decision overturning Roe v. Wade, telling "The Wall Street Journal" is a pretty good idea who leaked the draft of that decision.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And while investigators haven't been unable to identify the leaker, using a preponderance of the evidence standard, Justice Alito says he's certain of the motive, to prevent the draft from becoming the decision of the court.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020230502ej4s00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GLOBAL TENSIONS LAWMAKERS HOLD 'WAR GAMES' SIMULATING CHINESE INVASION OF TAIWAN\b0\par\par\uc2 1,186 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABCTHISWEEK\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, WAR, RESCUE, AMERICANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS RESCUE\par \par \uc2 MACE, SCOTUS, ABORTION, SPORTS, TRANS-ATHLETES, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 WARNER, ABORTION, SUDAN, INTEL, LEAK, FEINSTEIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 GUNS, VICTIMS, LUDWIG, GILLIS, HORWITZ, ARMSTRONG, ANDERSON\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, SCOTUS, TRUMP, 2024, BIDEN, POLL, DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM PRESS CONFERENCE FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, WAR GAMES, CHINA, TAIWAN, GALLAGHER, KRISHNAMOORTHI\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM INTERVIEW\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Taiwan makes their own judgments about their independence. We are not moving. We're not encouraging their being independent. We're not, that's their decision.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (CBS NEWS)\par \par \uc2 But would US forces defend the island?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Yes, if in fact there was an unprecedented attack.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY (CBS NEWS)\par \par \uc2 So, unlike Ukraine, to be clear, sir, US forces, US men and women would defend Taiwan in the event of a Chinese invasion?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden last fall saying for the first time that if China invades Taiwan, the US would send troops to defend it. It's an invasion many experts predict could happen in this decade. We got a fascinating inside look this week at how members of the House China Committee are gaming out war scenarios.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GLOBAL TENSIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAWMAKERS HOLD 'WAR GAMES' SIMULATING CHINESE INVASION OF TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the sky, on the sea, China staging combat exercises, a show of force, a warning that a potential blockade of Taiwan could be coming. The Chinese army even releasing this simulation of how they detect the nearby island, a self-governed democracy that China claims as its own territory. But on Capitol Hill this week, lawmakers staged their own simulation.\par \par \uc2 REP MICHAEL GALLAGHER (REP-WI) (CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 Tonight is not about a desire for war. It's certainly not about playing frivolous games.\par \par \uc2 REP RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (DEM-IL) (RANKING MEMBER/CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 It's about prevent wargames.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) China Committee Chairman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  and Ranking Member \uc2 Raja Krishnamoorthi\uc2  set up the exercise with the Center for New American Security.\par \par \uc2 STACIE PETTYJOHN (SR FELLOW & DIR OF DEFENSE PROGRAM/CTR FOR A NEW AMERICAN SECURITY)\par \par \uc2 This game is going to be a Chinese invasion of Taiwan set in 2027.\par \par \uc2 REP MICHAEL GALLAGHER (REP-WI) (CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 What exactly do we know right now about their mobilization effort?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Members from both sides of the aisle coming together, politics set aside. There's Virginia by Republican Robert Whitman and Massachusetts Democrat Jake Auchincloss, the members taking on the role of advisers to the President.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT HAVE WE COMMUNICATED TO EXPATS AND STUDENTS WHO ARE IN CHINA RIGHT NOW?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ANDR\u201\'00\'C9 CARSON (DEM) (INDIANA)\par \par \uc2 What have we communicated to expats and students who are in China right now?\par \par \uc2 STACIE PETTYJOHN (SR FELLOW & DIR OF DEFENSE PROGRAM/CTR FOR A NEW AMERICAN SECURITY)\par \par \uc2 This is part of what you get to decide.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That's Stacie Pettyjohn, a senior fellow from CNAS, acting as the Game Master.\par \par \uc2 REP MICHAEL GALLAGHER (REP-WI) (CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs can't pick up the phone and talk to his PLA counterpart.\par \par \uc2 STACIE PETTYJOHN (SR FELLOW & DIR OF DEFENSE PROGRAM/CTR FOR A NEW AMERICAN SECURITY)\par \par \uc2 Correct. Unfortunately, General Holmes is cut off.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Retired General Mike Holmes is playing the role of Chairman of the Joint Chiefs, laying out to members with military options there are.\par \par \uc2 MIKE HOLMES (RETIRED GENERAL)\par \par \uc2 The biggest benefit from fighting from a distance is you can preserve and protect your forces.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But diplomatic and economic options are on the table as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAVE ANY OF OUR ALLIES AGREED TO TAKE PART IN ECONOMIC ACTIVITIES WITH US AS A PRECURSOR TO THESE CONVERSATIONS?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ASHELY HINSON (REP) (IOWA)\par \par \uc2 Have any of our allies agreed to take part in activities with us as a precursor to the conversations?\par \par \uc2 REP RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (DEM-IL) (RANKING MEMBER/CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 Has there been any activity at the United Nations yet?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After debating how the US should react, lawmakers took a vote, announcing their move.\par \par \uc2 REP MICHAEL GALLAGHER (REP-WI) (CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 We will be standing with our friends in Taiwan. We have forces in the region that our pre-positioned. We're surging forces in the region. We are also prepared to impose maximum economic pressure in the event of an invasion, including sanctions against most major Chinese banks including kicking China of the SWIFT system.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But way a minute, Chinese authorities played by CNAS staffers quickly counter surging troops forcing a communications blackout in Taiwan and banning exports of electronic goods to the US.\par \par \uc2 CNAS STAFFER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 So, that means we are going after companies like \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Dell\uc2 , HP. You want a new iPhone? Guess what, you're not going to get it.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lots of questions, lots of lessons.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It was remarkable to see all of you there working together. It's not a view we often get. We, we see you in hearings. We see you on TV. But this is a group that is really working together.\par \par \uc2 REP MICHAEL GALLAGHER (REP-WI) (CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 We're trying to. And again, ultimately, we're hoping to generate creative ideas that can pass this Congress from what we can do to enhance deterrence. So, our hope is by doing something different, we generate more member engagement and more member ideas.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's obviously lessons learned from Ukraine. One of them was the US intelligence certainly believed Russia would be - be moving in.\par \par \uc2 REP MICHAEL GALLAGHER (REP-WI) (CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But really off the mark in terms of the power of Russia and the power of the troops. Do you trust the intelligence on this one, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 REP RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI (DEM-IL) (RANKING MEMBER/CHAIR HOUSE SELECT CMTE ON CHINA)\par \par \uc2 I trust the intelligence community and their assessment right now. I think you have to just prepare for the worst and hope for the best. I think that's just the way that we have to approach this scenario.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is what they are doing. Our thanks to the House China Committee. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's all for us today. Thanks for sharing part of your Sunday with us. Check out "World News Tonight," and have a great day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THISWEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOWNLOAD THE APP\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020230428ej4n00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ONE-ON-ONE WITH CONGRESSWOMAN NANCY MACE SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 1,813 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABCTHISWEEK\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, WAR, RESCUE, AMERICANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS RESCUE\par \par \uc2 MACE, SCOTUS, ABORTION, SPORTS, TRANS-ATHLETES, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And there was, of course, big news here in Washington this week. The Supreme Court deciding to keep in place access to the abortion drug Mifepristone, for now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The action blocked lower court rulings that would've scrapped the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's approval of the widely used pill and made it harder to obtain. But the decision guaranteed that the legal battle over the drug's availability will stay at the forefront of the national abortion debate, and that the issue could return to the highest court as early as next year.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Joining us now to discuss the fallout is Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace. Thanks very much for joining us this morning. You describe yourself as pro-life, but on this particular case you thought it should be thrown out completely.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Right, right. We...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What's your reaction to what the Supreme Court did?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Well, it was the right decision on Friday night by the Supreme Court. The judge's decision in Texas, this was a handpicked case with a handpicked judge to get this outcome. And when you look at the ruling in Texas, in part at least, it used a law that the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  in 1983 said was unconstitutional. So, the basis for his ruling, I argue, - was debunked and should not have been. And I totally disagreed with it.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and this isn't over. There are other cases moving through the court system. How do you see this ending up? How do you want this to end up?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BATTLES CONTINUE THROUGHOUT LEGAL SYSTEM\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Well, I want us to find some middle ground. As a Republican, a Conservative, constitutional Conservative who's pro-life, I saw what happened after Roe v. Wade because I represent a very purple district, as purple as this dress, and I saw the sentiment changed dramatically. And as Republicans, we need to read the room on this issue because the vast majority of folks are not in the extremes. And we just saw, you know, - a fetal heartbeat bill signed in the dead of night, recently in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICANS GRAPPLE WITH ABORTION DEBATE AHEAD OF 2024\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 There, in my home state of South Carolina, there was a small, it was a very small group of state legislators that filed a bill that would execute women who have abortions, and gave right, more rights to rapists than women who've been raped. That is the wrong message heading into '24. We're gonna, we're going to lose huge if we continue down this path of extremities. And finding that middle ground, the vast majority of people want some sort of gestational limits, not at, you know, not at nine months, but somewhere in the middle. They want exceptions for rape and incest. They want women to have access to birth control. These are all very common sense positions that we can take and still be pro-life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOES GOP RISK LOSING VOTERS OVER ABORTION DEBATE?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You heard Ron DeSantis talk about the six-week abortion ban. You're talking about other Republicans and finding a middle ground, but there's not lot of discussion about finding a middle ground in the Republican Party. In fact, there's not a lot of discussion about abortion in general. You've got DeSantis, you've got \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who, who wants to ban abortion. They'll talk about it. But generally, Republicans are staying away from, from it. Didn't help them in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 It hurt us in the midterms. We actually lost seats. As a Republican, I told my story on the campaign trail. I actually ran an ad about my own circumstances, I was raped as a teenager, and talked about how I would work and protect women who've been raped, girls who are victims of incest. And rather than by, winning by one point, like I did two years ago, we ended up winning by 14 points. And we made this issue part of our campaign because I want women to know you can be pro-life and be pro-woman. They're, they're not mutually exclusive.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 But we've have hidden away from this issue. We've have buried our head in the sand. We're afraid to talk about it. Because we're afraid, we wanna, we want to go to the extreme corners of this issue, but that's not where the vast majority of Americans are right now. And we have to show compassion especially to victims who've been raped. The Florida bill mandated that women who are raped, to get that exception, had to report it to the, to the police, had to get evidence at a hospital.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, based on my own experiences, it took me a week before I was actually able to tell somebody what happened to me. And by that time, there is no evidence. There's nothing you can collect at that point. And, you know, that puts heavy, heavy restrictions on, on these victims. And there are millions of women everywhere who've gone through this. I'm not the only one.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE PASSES BILL TO BAR TRANS ATHLETES FROM WOMEN'S SPORTS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I, I want to return to something you voted on this week and something you feel strongly about. You voted Thursday to bar transgender athletes from women's sports. Forty professional athletes say that is a mistake. Why do you feel differently?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Well, I mean, this is the left's new war on women. I mean, both sides, whether it's the extreme right on abortion, or extreme left on biological men and women's sports, we have come so far. I don't want to see biological men take away the achievements of women and girls. I, I, I told a story on Thursday. I was molested by a friend of one of my coaches at the age of 14. I was raped by a classmate at the age of 16. Before a girl ever sets foot in the pool, or on the court, or on the field, she's already in a very vulnerable position.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 And we've worked hundreds of years for our achievements. It took 100 years for the state of South Carolina, 100 after women's suffrage to get their first Republican woman in Congress when I won in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I - want to go back to those athletes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why, why do you know more than those athletes?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Well, if you read the stories...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They, they came up through, through youth sports.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Well, Riley Gaines, okay? There are also other athletes we've met along the way. There was a story last week in Wisconsin where 14-year-old girls were forced to share a locker room and showers with an 18-year-old boy, biological male, who's, I guess, a trans girl now, but exposed himself in the showers to these young, underaged girls. My girl is about to be 14. I can't imagine if there were men in her locker room exposing themselves. And you hear these stories, you hear these stories more frequently as we go on, and it, and it's wrong. It is a war on women. It's a different war on women.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's obviously wrong to expose yourself.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 But it's still a war on women.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT OF GOV. COX'S STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But I, I want to talk about the Republican - governor of Utah, \uc2 Spencer Cox\uc2 . He vetoed a similar bill in his state. It was eventually overruled. But this is what the Governor said when data showed only four transgender kids playing high school sports in Utah. "Four kids who are just trying to find some friends and feel like they are part of something, four kids trying to get through each day. Rarely has so much fear and anger been directed at so few. I don't understand what they are going through or why they feel the way they do but I want them to live." Obviously talking about the extremely high suicide rates among the transgender population.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Right. And, and look, I'm an, I'm a pro-LGBTQ Republican. That's not what this is about. We don't want anyone committing suicide because we've taken this position. To conflate the two is, is a radical and extreme position to take. But we want, what we do wanna do is protect women and girls. I mean, it took 154 years before the Citadel admitted women. And I was the first female graduate of that college. You see women whose achievements, whether it's a, a medal in an athletic event, or they're losing scholarships, or they're being exposed, underage, in a locker room. Those things are wrong.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 And though it doesn't happen all day, every day, but we still want to protect our women and girls. And when you talk to parents of these girls, when you talk to the girls directly, they're very angry about it. They're very upset about it. We don't want to keep anyone from competing, I mean, but these are biological men. They're much stronger. They shouldn't be in the locker rooms and you hear the stories and they're real. It's not a, it's not a fantasy. It's not conspiracy theory. These are happening. These things are happening to women and girls across the country.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks very much for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020230428ej4n00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 2,990 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABCTHISWEEK\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, WAR, RESCUE, AMERICANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS RESCUE\par \par \uc2 MACE, SCOTUS, ABORTION, SPORTS, TRANS-ATHLETES, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 WARNER, ABORTION, SUDAN, INTEL, LEAK, FEINSTEIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 GUNS, VICTIMS, LUDWIG, GILLIS, HORWITZ, ARMSTRONG, ANDERSON\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, SCOTUS, TRUMP, 2024, BIDEN, POLL, DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM PRESS CONFERENCE FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Does the President have any plans to mark the fourth anniversary of his announcement of his 2020 campaign?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE (WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 That's a - that is very good.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I had to try. I just (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE (WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 "I had to try." I don't have anything else beyond the week ahead that I just laid out. But I really appreciate the effort. That was very good.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was ABC News new chief White House correspondent Mary Bruce pressing the White House on President Biden's reelection announcement, which would come as soon as this week. Let's bring in the Roundtable, former Justice Department spokesperson \uc2 Sarah Isgur\uc2 , former North Dakota Senator Heidi Heitkamp, ABC News senior national correspondent Terry Moran, and "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " congressional reporter Marianna Sotomayor. Welcome to all of you this morning. We've got a lot to get to, and Heidi, I'm gonna start with you. And I do wanna turn back to the Supreme Court decision on Mifepristone. A win for Democrats now, but what do you see is coming next?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I, I, I think that it's going to be extraordinarily difficult for the Republican Party to justify reversing a decision that was made to 20 years ago. And every day that this issue is on the front page, that it seems like everything is so extreme on that side is a day that they aren't talking about inflation, they aren't talking about the issues they want to talk about. They're talking about an issue that is a net loser for them in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Sara, you were at the Justice Department. You are a lawyer. When you looked at this decision and only two justices dissented publicly, Alito, and and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , we don't really know what else went on in there and we've got all these other cases coming up.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. I mean, first of all, to the extent that the justices thought they were gonna get out of the abortion business after Dobbs, clearly not the case. There are a lot of these similar type cases percolating at the lower courts. Second, though, you know, it feels like the courts get attacked whenever a decision comes out the way people don't like. And I just think it's worth noting, like, remember this, remember Bostock, that it's not simply that the courts lack integrity just because you don't like the decision.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And here, frankly, at the Supreme Court level, this decision wasn't actually about abortion. It was about who has the injury to bring this lawsuit in the first place, and Conservatives should be a little careful. If these doctors can bring a challenge on Mifepristone, I don't see why a similar group of doctors could bring a challenge against gun manufacturers, this idea that you're injured by being an emergency room doctor helping patients with something.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Terry, when the justices ruled to overturn Roe versus Wade ten months ago, they indicated they wanted to be done with the issue, writing in the majority opinion, "The authority to regulate abortion is returned to the people and their elected representatives." Is that realistic?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BATTLE CONTINUE THROUGH LEGAL SYSTEM\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, it's not happening. And that was Justice Alito. He said it again and again. And yet, what they're seeing is that there is something in some of these courts. I think what this stay was, was really the Supreme Court, at least five members, saying, enough is enough with the craziness of this case, as Sarah described it. The way it came into court was somewhat shocking. There was something reckless and wild about it.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I think, you know, what you see in the pharmaceutical industry and in other industries is wait a minute, if, if we're gonna get cases like this where any Tom, Dick or Harry can waltz into federal court and overturn a federal agency's decision based on all the proper process and science 20 years standing, what's next? And I think that was very alarming. The Court is gonna see more of these because it's clear there are Republican judges willing to do it.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and let's talk about the politics of this more, Marianna. Republicans, Sarah pointed out, Heidi pointed out, do kind of struggle to find this middle ground when they talk. You heard Nancy Mace, but she is not in the majority.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOES GOP RISK LOSING VOTERS OVER ABORTION DEBATE?\par \par \uc2 MARIANNA SOTOMAYOR (WASHINGTON POST CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 Right, Nancy Mace is someone who is in the House Republican majority but is absolutely a minority vote, voice on this. When you're talking to House Republicans, they simply don't want to talk about this. And a number of things that they wanted to bring up before the midterm election, like banning abortions at 15 weeks at the federal level, that bill is nonexistent. You will not hear Republicans talking about that.\par \par \uc2 MARIANNA SOTOMAYOR (WASHINGTON POST CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 And it is also an issue already with a number of these Republican candidates who have announced for president and those who haven't because they also aren't necessarily speaking about it. They're saying, you know, this is state rights. But even that is really annoying those pro-life groups. And I think the tricky part for Republicans is you could take a position in the primary that might be a little bit more extreme. How is that gonna fare for you in the general? And that is really a calculus that Republicans are struggling to figure out...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. With, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , even his...\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You see a big difference between...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, his, yeah.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...Republican primary voters and Republican-leaning general election voters. There was a poll out from Gallup recently that said now 49 percent of Americans do not identify with either political party. As the political party shrinks, they get weaker on the one hand. They have less control over who their standard-bearers are which is dangerous to some extent. But also, it means that concentration of Republican primary voters don't look like general election voters. It puts the Republican Party in an iffy position.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has now told us what the theory of his case is, right? He now has said, "Look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is wrong on abortion. I am going to be the pro-life - vote in the Republican primary." He figures that there's going to be enough of that percentage of Republican votes that is going to lead him to victory. But an additional, kind of, fallout from this was \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  basically saying, "I was duped by this judge in Texas. I didn't get the right information." I wonder what the reform is going to be on vetting these judges, because the judge in Texas was way off what he told that nominating committee he would do.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT OF STATEMENT, \uc2 SUSAN B. ANTHONY\uc2  PRO-LIFE AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But - and I, I wanted to talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this week, his spokesman said he believes abortion should be left up to the states. The \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  list, an anti-abortion group, blasted that position as morally indefensible. And the group's president said, "We will oppose any presidential candidate who refuses to embrace at a minimum a 15-week national standard. Holding to the position that is exclusively up to the states is an abdication of responsibility by anyone elected to federal office." This is a tough...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME ANTI-ABORTION RIGHTS GROUPS SEEK FEDERAL BAN\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...a tough position for those candidates to be in, Terry.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But the logic of the Republican Party is a national ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right? Because the ratchet in the primaries is that whoever is gonna take a more pro-life anti-abortion rights position is going to get more votes in those primaries 'cause the base controls it. And the logic of the position is that there needs to be a national ban on abortion. That's what these judges in Texas are doing, that they can use the power of the federal judiciary to stop abortion nationwide. That is political disaster because most Americans have always been in the vast model middle on this subject.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But when it comes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it feels like 2015 all over again. Who's leading the party? The base voters or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leading the base voters. So, I wouldn't be surprised if he's the general election nominee, that \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  gets on board like everyone else.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but think of, think...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And he certainly believe...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Think of what's happening in the cascade effect in state elections. You saw that in Wisconsin, where a, where a liberal judge, so, so now this issue cuts across the board and it has a huge effect on who's sitting in the state legislature, who has that governor's seat, who has the mayor's seat, and who has the Supreme Court seat.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECENT ELECTIONS SUGGEST ABORTION MOTIVATING DEM TURNOUT\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now let's talk about the President. Let's talk about 2024. Marianna, we heard the White House dance around this announcement, but they're whispering, it's going to be Tuesday. It's going to be pretty low-key the way Biden announces. How do you, how do you see that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: BIDEN MAY ANNOUNCE 2024 BID AS SOON AS THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 MARIANNA SOTOMAYOR (WASHINGTON POST CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, you know, a couple of my colleagues have been reporting about the days leading up to this announcement. And it really has been a low-key affair, really meeting with his wife, a number of advisers, to put out a video which seems to have been filmed out in Wilmington, Delaware. It's possible his headquarters will be there instead of Philadelphia. We obviously see the President going there a lot. But they really want to keep the focus on what he is doing as president.\par \par \uc2 MARIANNA SOTOMAYOR (WASHINGTON POST CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 They, Democrats largely, you can even say this on the congressional side, just want to have the Republicans duke it out in the primary, have Biden kind of be in the backdrop talking about his own achievements. So, it is likely that it's probably going to take that kind of tone.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Terry, do you expect everyone to, all the Democrats to suddenly rally around the President?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I'll, I'll leave that to, to our, our Democratic former official here. My own sense is that most Americans don't want to vote for an 81-year-old man for president.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AP POLL: ROUGHLY HALF OF DEMS WANT BIDEN TO RUN IN '24\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Neither do they really wanna vote for a 77-year-old man for president. They'd like, they'd, they'd like to go to the next generation. And I think part of the modesty is the, is the Biden and White House operation aware that people are uneasy with his age.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT OF STATEMENT FROM WSJ OPINION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and to that point, Heidi, the "Wall Street Journal" Editorial Board had a very strong statement about it, saying, "The public understands what Mr. Biden apparently won't admit, that electing an octogenarian in obvious decline for another four years could be an historic mistake. It's impossible to know Mr. Biden's real physical and mental state because the White House goes to great lengths to hide it. But his decline is clear to anyone who isn't willfully blind."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WSJ EDITORIAL BOARD: BIDEN 'IN OBVIOUS DECLINE'\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, and, and - the real issue here is when you don't compare Biden to, you know, kind of the perfect candidate. You - compare him to who the likely nominee is. And I think that there's a comfort level as we move into this that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  probably will be the nominee. This will - be a repeat of 2020. Biden has a lot of things to run on. He's done a lot of good policy things that both Progressives and Moderate Republicans like. And so, I think there isn't gonna be a lot of opposition within the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But I also think that the attention that we're gonna see Democrats expose is these false lines among the Republicans. So, I mean, you know, there is going to be this question, right? And rightfully so. But I think when you look at what the, what the administration has in fact accomplished in the last four years is pretty impressive. It's a pretty impressive record. And so, let's see how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Sarah, let's move on to the GOP side. This wasn't the greatest week for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Comes to DC, a rare appearance, only to find at about half of the Republicans in, in Florida, or from Florida, in Congress, are going for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FL GOP REPS BACK TRUMP OVER DESANTIS FOR WH\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I mean, look at, I think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's team is making a smart decision in the sense that they wanna replay of 2020, where he didn't really do much, say much, keep all the focus on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and make it a referendum on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That worked really well for them last time. I have to say, where I think they are making a mistake is assuming that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the weaker Republican candidate. Feels very \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , you know, trying to sort of egg on, you know, this is our chosen candidate. Democrats, of course, have played in many Republican primaries so far, in the last few years, to try to pick their candidates.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is not clear to me at all that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be the weaker candidate against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . As far as \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is concerned, I think Republicans, and this is true for Tim Scott and Nikki Haley and all of these guys, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  changed the scoring in Republican primaries. It's like we got rid of the three pointer, and yet here are all these Republican sitting at the three-point line, making these difficult shots and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just says the thing.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a dunking contest right now. Start dunking. Don't say you want a drama-free president and then everyone, you know, sitting on this roundtable's like, "Ooh, that was an attack on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ." No. Say, "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a drama queen." Like, you need to actually bring it to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if you wanna beat him.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAKING STOCK OF THE 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CAMPAIGN\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And to that point, Marianna, we have about one minute left. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  this week, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is, of course, an ABC News contributor, often sits on this roundtable, but toying with a presidential run. He was at a New England college town hall this week and basically said "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has failed us." Do you think that will resonate?\par \par \uc2 MARIANNA SOTOMAYOR (WASHINGTON POST CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have seen so many attacks against Trump and it really hasn't. It seems like Christie's position is to just go in and constantly be saying these things. Maybe that could resonate in early states to some voters. But, at the end of the day, in this kind of primary climate where we've seen Trump just, again and again and again, be underestimated and come out on top, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let me just say it is very early. It is still April in 2023. We have a very long time to go. But thank you all for joining us. We're gonna be talking about this for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Coming up, as tensions rise between China and Taiwan, we were on Capitol Hill for a late-night wargame. It was bipartisanship at work, next.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020230428ej4n00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ONE-ON-ONE WITH SENATOR MARK WARNER SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 1,465 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABCTHISWEEK\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, WAR, RESCUE, AMERICANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS RESCUE\par \par \uc2 MACE, SCOTUS, ABORTION, SPORTS, TRANS-ATHLETES, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 WARNER, ABORTION, SUDAN, INTEL, LEAK, FEINSTEIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're joined by Democratic Senator Mark Warner, chair of the Senate Intelligence Committee. Thanks for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I, I want to go back to the Supreme Court's decision to allow full access to the abortion pill. How do you read the Court on this? In regards to the other cases coming up, what do you think is gonna happen there?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure how to react. I, I do know that it was the judge's decision in Texas. I think it was crazy. The notion that you would take a drug that has been used safely for more than two decades and somehow than take that away from availability. You know, I, frankly, think this is an issue that women's healthcare choices ought to be made by women. And the idea of this judge so radically intervening with a safe procedure, you know, frankly, not only about women's health care choices, but it undermines the very integrity of our \uc2 FDA\uc2  process. And I think the Court got it right, but who knows where their ultimate decision will be?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are, are you worried about abortion access for your constituents? Governor Glenn Youngkin made it clear his administration will prioritize - limiting access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VA GOV GLENN YOUNGKIN PUSHES 15-WEEK ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 I think that is not where the vast majority of Virginians are at. And I think, as you see these moves towards even a six-week ban in Florida, that voters, I think, will overwhelmingly reject those kind of policies.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and I want to turn now to, to Sudan, quickly if we can. You are on the Intelligence Committee. What have you seen about what is going to happen there next and, and in particular those American citizens?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US MILITARY EVACUATES DIPLOMATS FROM SUDAN\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think we first of all, we ought to commend the military and the intelligence community for getting our diplomatic personnel out. That was a dangerous mission to take them out by helicopter. I think we've made, been very clear literally since 2021 that Americans should not travel to Sudan. The vast majority of those, the numbers have been bandied around 16,000 are dual nationals, Sudanese Americans.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS OF AMERICAN CITIZENS REMAIN IN SUDAN\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 Those individuals who were there on aid missions, there is actually a \uc2 UN\uc2  effort now to get people from Khartoum out over the land corridor. And I know we are working with a lot of international partners. In this case, partners from the Middle East, even, I believe, working with, with China and Europeans to say those aid workers, we need to find a safe way to get, to get them out. In the meantime, though, they do need to shelter in place until those conveys are put in place.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENTAGON INTEL LEAK SPARKS DEBATE OVER CLASSIFICATION SYSTEM\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and let's talk about those intelligence documents. It's been going on for several weeks, but it seems to get worse and worse. More documents leaked. The Pentagon apparently unaware that they were leaked many, many, many months ago. When you look at this, who should you point the finger at?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, Martha, this is a problem that we shouldn't be totally surprised at. We've known for a long time, we way over classify documents. Matter of fact, the default position is always to classify, number one. Number two, we don't have a single entity in charge. So in many cases, what may be classified within the intelligence community may be different types of documents than the DOD. The third thing is once we get to that highest level of classification, we maybe have too many folks taking a look at them, over four million people with clearances. So let's classify less...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A computer technician.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 Well, a computer technician. If this had been at another entity, another agency, like the NSA, where, unfortunately, we've had leaks in the past, there would've been internal controls that would've said, you just can't copy that many documents. We need to make sure we've got similar internal controls across the whole system. I believe we need somebody fully in charge of the classification process. And I believe for those most classified documents, there ought to be a smaller universe.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRMAN \uc2 WHO\uc2  ALLEGEDLY LEAKED US INTEL FACES PROSECUTION\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 And this individual, which literally just an IT tech, there's no reason that that person should have been able to see the full document. You may have to see the header, but the actual contents, there is ways to make this safer. We've known this has been a problem. We actually have to have, I believe, Congress intervene and we have to do a better job. And frankly, this individual, I think, clearly if, if it's proven to be the case that he was the leaker, he needs to be prosecuted to the fullest extent of the law.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAWMAKERS CALL FOR INTELLIGENCE REFORMS AFTER PENTAGON LEAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are, are you satisfied with what you have heard from the administration? I know in the very early days the Pentagon was saying, look, we have a safety things in place so this can't happen. Clearly, that did not work. boxes can happen pretty clearly that did not work. So, are you satisfied with what you have already heard in your briefing?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: PIERRE THOMAS REPORTS ON THE LATEST STRING OF 'WRONG ADDRESS' INCIDENTS AND THE VICTIMS SHOT FOR INNOCENT MISTAKES\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 I think this is still an evolving case. The one thing that we've got to do a better job on, and I've been a big advocate of security clearance reform. We can't go to other end of the spectrum where somebody has to wait a year or two before they get a security clearance if they want to go work for something like the CIA. But when we say we're gonna do going to do security clearance reform, we then have to put in place something called continuous vetting, so you're vetted along the way on an ongoing basis.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 But it really raises really hard questions. You know, should that vetting of course it, you should be able to look at your public \uc2 Facebook\uc2  postings. But when we're talking something that's a private chatroom with a so-called discord site, that raises a whole series of other questions I don't think we've sorted through enough.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN FEINSTEIN FACING PRESSURE TO RESIGN AMID HEALTH ISSUES\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and very quickly, if you will, Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 , your Intelligence Committee colleague, has been away from the Senate since February with, with shingles. Should she resign?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 Listen, I'm hopeful that Dianne will return as soon as possible. I've served with her on the Intelligence Committee. She's been a great senator. But my hope is that she'll get back to work as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks very much for joining us this morning, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK WARNER (DEM-VA) (SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The Roundtable is coming up. Plus, after a week of wrong address mistakes leading to a series of shootings across the country, Pierre Thomas and our panel analyze the state of gun violence in America. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABCTHISWEEK\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020230428ej4n00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MULTIPLE SHOOTINGS OCCUR AFTER INNOCENT ERRORS SERIES OF SEEMINGLY UNPROVOKED SHOOTINGS SHOCK COUNTRY\b0\par\par\uc2 1,910 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABCTHISWEEK\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, WAR, RESCUE, AMERICANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS RESCUE\par \par \uc2 MACE, SCOTUS, ABORTION, SPORTS, TRANS-ATHLETES, \uc2 COX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 WARNER, ABORTION, SUDAN, INTEL, LEAK, FEINSTEIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT, HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 GUNS, VICTIMS, LUDWIG, GILLIS, HORWITZ, ARMSTRONG, ANDERSON\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 GWENDOLYN GRANT (CEO URBAN LEAGUE OF GREATER KANSAS CITY)\par \par \uc2 We are challenged just walking while Black, and now we can't even walk up to someone's door and ring a doorbell without fear of being murdered.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF JEFFREY MURPHY (WASHINGTON COUNTY NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 They turned around, were heading down the driveway, obviously no threat if they are driving away. And that's when Mr. Monahan chose to fire at the vehicles.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 ALEX STANISZEWSKI (GASTON COUNTY NORTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 It's an absolute travesty that what should've just been a mild conversation with a child about anything turned into someone getting shot.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) From Missouri to New York to North Carolina, a string of innocent mistakes transformed into tragedies. What's fueling this fear and paranoia? And what justifies a Stand Your Ground self-defense? We'll speak with our panel of experts after this report from chief justice correspondent Pierre Thomas.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GUNS IN AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MULTIPLE RECENT SHOOTINGS OCCUR AFTER INNOCENT ERRORS\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This week, a series of maddening tragedies.\par \par \uc2 AUDIO CLIP OF EMERGENCY CALL\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY DISPATCHER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Somebody is saying their child has been shot.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A family shot because a basketball rolled onto someone else's property.\par \par \uc2 KINSLEY WHITE (VICTIM OF SHOOTING)\par \par \uc2 Why did you shoot my daddy and me?\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Two cheerleaders shot because one got into the wrong car after practice.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "HE PULLED OUT A GUN AND HE JUST STARTED SHOOTING AT ALL OF US."\par \par \uc2 CHEERLEADER (VICTIM OF SHOOTING)\par \par \uc2 He pulled out a gun and then he just started shooting at all of us.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A 16-year-old boy critically wounded for simply knocking on the wrong door.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 KLINT LUDWIG (GRANDSON OF ALLEGED SHOOTER)\par \par \uc2 I feel like a lot of people of our generation are caught up in this 24-hour news cycle of fear and paranoia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SERIES OF SEEMINGLY UNPROVOKED SHOOTINGS SHOCK COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And a young woman fatally shot when the car she was in simply drove up the wrong driveway.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM NEWS CONFERENCE\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GILLIS (FATHER OF KAYLIN GILLIS)\par \par \uc2 For this man to sit on his porch and fire at a car with no threat.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We were warned about this disturbing trend of spontaneous gun violence last fall by one of America's top police officers.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM "THIS WEEK" INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROWN (FORMER CHICAGO POLICE SUPERINTENDENT)\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is the inability to resolve personal conflicts of, that, you know, that escalated to gun violence.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This should be no surprise. America for a variety of reasons, culturally, and because of interpretations of the Constitution, has embraced firearms like no other country on the planet. The numbers tell the story of the country's voracious appetite for firearms. In the last three years, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has conducted over 100 million background checks as people sought to buy guns. And there are an estimated 400 million guns already in circulation in the United States, more guns than people.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM "THIS WEEK" INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 JOSH HORWITZ (CO-DIRECTOR JOHNS HOPKINS CENTER FOR GUN VIOLENCE SOLUTIONS)\par \par \uc2 The problems with guns is fueling what we're seeing. We are seeing the idea that we are a shoot-first culture. Everybody seems to be afraid. They've been told to be afraid.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW DOES MENTAL HEALTH FACTOR INTO GUN VIOLENCE?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLL: 18% OF US HOUSEHOLDS BOUGHT A GUN DURING PANDEMIC\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So many guns and so many opportunities for them to be in the hands of people who should not have them, like the recent mass shootings done by people clearly struggling with mental health issues. A recent surge in gun sales coincided with the timeline of the pandemic. Some analysts say it was a witch's brew moment, more guns into society at a moment when people faced economic uncertainty and anxiety, the impact of it all clearly lingering.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HORWITZ (CO-DIRECTOR JOHNS HOPKINS CENTER FOR GUN VIOLENCE SOLUTIONS)\par \par \uc2 With all these guns, science tells us what we saw this week is predictable.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With no end in sight. For "This Week," Pierre Thomas, ABC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Pierre. Let's take a closer look at the issue now with two experts on gun violence and law enforcement, Apex North Carolina Police Chief Jason Armstrong who previously led the police force in Ferguson, Missouri, and Allison Anderman, senior counsel and director of local policy at the Giffords Law Center. Thanks to both of you for joining us this morning. And Chief, I, I want to start with this. You saw those terrible events in the past few weeks. You've seen a lot of bad things happen. Did this shock you? Why is this happening now?\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JASON ARMSTRONG (APEX NORTH CAROLINA POLICE DEPARTMENT)\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, it did not shock me. We've seen a lot of violence across the country, especially coming out of the pandemic. We've seen an uptick in, in gun violence. And, and with everything that we've seen, it's just more and more people are on the edge. More and more people are, are fearful of things happening to them, and we see unfortunately more and more people are quicker to resort to gun violence as opposed to try to work out whatever issue they're dealing with.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Allison, you track state firearm safety and legislation. Are their laws on the books, we mentioned stand your ground, and you have concealed carry that contribute to this?\par \par \uc2 ALLISON ANDERMAN (SR COUNSEL & DIR OF LOCAL POLICY GIFFORDS LAW CENTER)\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, yes. We know that stand your ground laws which upend centuries of common law on self-defense and allow people to carry guns outside of the home and really take another life even if they could've retreated safely from a confrontation, are increasing numbers of justifiable and unjustifiable homicides.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOOTINGS RAISE QUESTIONS ABOUT STAND-YOUR-GROUND LAWS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Chief, do you see the stand your ground law making a difference? Are people aware of it? Is it in their mind all the time?\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JASON ARMSTRONG (APEX NORTH CAROLINA POLICE DEPARTMENT)\par \par \uc2 From what I've seen, I, I don't think a lot of people are aware of it until there's an incident that happens where it becomes a topic of discussion. A lot of people in, in the course of my career that I've come across, they're, they're - rarely are they familiar with all of the laws that are on the books or how those laws are, are applied. And so, I think, you know, a big contributor to what we're seeing right now is just, you know, people are, are not able to do with conflict from other people and, and gun violence is what they're resulting to. And I think, you know, the laws applying to it are, are secondary in what's going on in the heat of the moment.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Allison, gun manufacturers and gun lobby groups often market their firearm ownership in terms of, of self-defense. And in 2022, the \uc2 NRA\uc2  super PAC ran digital ads against Democratic Senate candidates in battleground states, including Mark Kelly there in Arizona, and targeting your group specifically. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIPS FROM CAMPAIGN ADS\par \par \uc2 CAMPAIGN AD ANNOUNCER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Kelly cofounded a radical gun-control group promoting anti-gun Liberals across the country, making Arizona less safe. Don't be left defenseless.\par \par \uc2 CAMPAIGN AD ANNOUNCER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz is committed to keeping our communities safe. Protect your family. Violent crime is out of control.\par \par \uc2 CAMPAIGN AD ANNOUNCER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's time for a change, Nevada. Defend your right to self-defense.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTORAL POLITICS SHAPING HEATED US GUN DEBATE\par \par \uc2 ALLISON ANDERMAN (SR COUNSEL & DIR OF LOCAL POLICY GIFFORDS LAW CENTER)\par \par \uc2 I think there is a narrative in this country being pushed by gun, the gun industry and certain legislators that a person needs to be armed in public at all times to be safe. That they have a constitutional right to shoot someone at the slightest provocation. And to Chief Armstrong's point, they may not know about stand your ground laws specifically, but they certainly hear the messaging in the press and in political campaigns that I think are enabling this violence.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Chief, just look at this in a bigger way. What, what really needs to happen here to stop this kind of reaction, to have people drive up your driveway be shot? What, what do you see is the solution there? What do you tell the people of Apex, North Carolina?\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JASON ARMSTRONG (APEX NORTH CAROLINA POLICE DEPARTMENT)\par \par \uc2 I think a big part of the solution is education and having those - having those conversations with people in your community and help educate them on, you know, proper gun ownership and, and how they can, you know, have - a gun but also be mindful in, in how they, they use that gun. 'Cause we, we run through these scenarios all the time and we, we know that more guns are out there. More people in the communities have guns and...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you're dealing with emotion, too.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JASON ARMSTRONG (APEX NORTH CAROLINA POLICE DEPARTMENT)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. And so, we talk about if we respond to an incident and people have guns, we don't know who the assailant is and, and who is a, a lawful gun owner that's trying to, to stop a situation. And so, those are extra scenarios that we have to run through as law enforcement in how we respond to scenes and how we respond to incidents. And so, the more conversations that we can have on the front end with people in our community and help educate them, you know, on proper gun ownership and, and what the laws say and how they apply, I think, is how we'll see some better end results in some of these situations.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks so much. And thanks both of you for joining us this morning, a very complicated issue.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Coming up, President Biden is preparing to announce his reelection bid as Donald Trump reasserts his dominance over the Republican Party. The Powerhouse Roundtable weighs in next.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABCTHISWEEK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The Roundtable's here, ready to go. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020230428ej4n00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION ON THE BATTLE? GOP PRESIDENTIAL HOPEFULS GRAPPLE WITH ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS:\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT EVACUATION FROM US EMBASSY\par \par \uc2 EVACUATION, SUDAN, EMBASSY, CHINOOK, SIMS, MULROY, VIOLENCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT EVACUATION FROM US EMBASSY\par \par \uc2 AA, FIRES, BIRD STRIKE, PASSENGERS, MAYDAY, \uc2 FAA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PANIC ON PLANES\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, WIND, HAIL, TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE STORM THREAT\par \par \uc2 BAD BATH & BEYOND, BANKRUPTCY, OPERATIONS, LEVY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RETAIL GIANT FILES FOR BANKRUPTCY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, 2024, GOP, ABORTION, PENCE, MACE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ON THE BALLOT?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jaclyn, thank you. The countdown to 2024 is on. Sources tell ABC News President Biden could announce a re-election bid as soon as this week. Republican presidential candidates and potential contenders testing the waters in Iowa, was gearing up for the GOP caucuses. Former Vice President Pence, further distancing himself from his former boss on the issue of abortion. Here's ABC's Jay O'Brien.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ON THE BALLOT?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOP PRESIDENTIAL HOPEFULS GRAPPLE WITH ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, with sources telling ABC News President Biden could announce his re-election bid as early as this Tuesday, Republicans hoping to challenge Biden, making their case to voters in the crucial state of Iowa.\par \par \uc2 SEN TIME SCOTT (REP) (NORTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 Not on my watch.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Speaking at an event popular with evangelicals, hosted by the faith and freedom coalition.\par \par \uc2 BECKY MCKIBBEN (IOWA VOTER)\par \par \uc2 The Midwest needs to have some say in what's going on around in our country.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just days after the Supreme Court ruled to temporarily preserve access to the abortion pill Mifepristone, the issue top of mind.\par \par \uc2 IOWA VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I was thankful that the Supreme Court overturned Roe V. Wade earlier and sent the decision back to the states.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appearing by video. The current GOP frontrunner now facing backlash from a prominent anti-abortion group after his spokesperson told "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," Trump believes abortion restrictions 'Should be decided at the state level." But former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who today said he could decide on a presidential bid as early as June, breaking with his onetime boss, calling for a nationwide 15 week ban.\par \par \uc2 FMR VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I don't agree with the former President, he says it is a states only issue. I think we have an opportunity to advance the sanctity of life, move it ever closer to the center of American law.\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some Republicans, though, pleading caution.\par \par \uc2 REP NANCY MACE (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 As Republicans, we need to read the room on this issue because the vast majority of folks are not in the extremes. We're going to lose huge if we continue down this path of extremities.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jay O'Brien joins us now. And, Jay, what more are we learning about President Biden's possible re-election announcement this week?\par \par \uc2 JAY O'BRIEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Linsey, a lot of attention on a possible Tuesday announcement, because that would mark the four year anniversary of the launch of President Biden's 2020 campaign. Sources telling ABC News, the White House already reaching out to top Democratic donors, and that that campaign kickoff would likely come in the form of a video message, Linsey.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020230425ej4n00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "OUR AMERICA: TROUBLE ON TAP" SNEAK PEEK NORTH CAROLINA RESIDENT SHARES HER QUEST FOR SAFE DRINKING WATER\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATION, WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, PARENTING, GRUNDEY\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2 , KYRIACOU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO CORONATION DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FMR COMMUNICATIONS SECRETARY ON HOW \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2  IS GETTING ROYALLY READY\par \par \uc2 EARTH DAY, "OUR AMERICA", OLINGER, OLSON\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now, we're going to turn it over to Earth Day. Oh, I like that sound. One North Carolina resident's quest for safe drinking water.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "OUR AMERICA: TROUBLE ON TAP" SNEAK PEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH CAROLINA RESIDENT SHARES HER QUEST FOR SAFE DRINKING WATER\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, the story is part of a special produced by National Geographic, ABC News and ABC-owned stations. Here's a sneak peek at "Our America: Trouble on Tap" special. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 CARROL OLINGER (FAYETTEVILLE NC RESIDENT AND ACTIVIST)\par \par \uc2 I didn't know I was drinking poisonous water. I didn't know when I was coming from a long day at work and I need to fill up my jug, I'm still drinking poisonous water. My name Carrol Olinger. I've lived in Fayetteville Hope Mill since 1995.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAYETTEVILLE, NORTH CAROLINA, WHERE THE CHEMOURS PLANT IS LOCATED, IS ONE OF MOST DISADVANTAGED COMMUNITIES IN THE UNITED STATES, ACCORDING TO CENSUS DATA FROM WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL ON ENVIRONMENTAL QUALITY. IT FACES HIGH RATES OF POLLUTION, HEALTH RISKS AND POVERTY.\par \par \uc2 ERIK D OLSON (SENIOR STRATEGIST NATIONAL RESOURCES DEFENSE COUNCIL)\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, safe drinking water in the United States sometimes depends on your zip code. We should not have to rely on being wealthy or being in a community that happens to have a lot of resources in order to have a water that's safe. In low-income communities and communities of color and Native American communities, we're seeing that they do not have safe drinking water. And that needs to be fixed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 49% OF ZIP CODES WHERE PFAS ARE DETECTED IN WATER SITES ARE IN ZIP CODES WHERE THE MEDIAN HOUSEHOLD INCOME WAS BELOW THE 2020 NATIONAL AVERAGE OF $67,521.\par \par \uc2 CARROL OLINGER (FAYETTEVILLE NC RESIDENT AND ACTIVIST)\par \par \uc2 I call myself an accidental activist. What do we want?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Clean water.\par \par \uc2 CARROL OLINGER (FAYETTEVILLE NC RESIDENT AND ACTIVIST)\par \par \uc2 When do we want it?\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Now.\par \par \uc2 CARROL OLINGER (FAYETTEVILLE NC RESIDENT AND ACTIVIST)\par \par \uc2 I don't think that I was a cheerleader for environmental justice, environmental health, but I think I am now because of the situation I accidently fell into. Unfortunately, it's hurting communities that look like me, underserved communities. I think that the black and brown, Latino, Indian communities, when it hits fully, it's going to hurt them the most because they're not going to have an option. It's scary.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, without admitting to any violations, the \uc2 Chemours Company\uc2  has taken steps to clean up the pollution and agreed to a $12-million fine in 2018. "Our America: Trouble on Tap" is streaming right now on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . An important special there.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ALL EARS FOR NEW MUSICAL "SHUCKED" WHY THEATERGOERS KEEP COMING BACK TO THIS PUN-PACKED PRODUCTION\b0\par\par\uc2 994 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATION, WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, PARENTING, GRUNDEY\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2 , KYRIACOU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO CORONATION DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FMR COMMUNICATIONS SECRETARY ON HOW \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2  IS GETTING ROYALLY READY\par \par \uc2 EARTH DAY, "OUR AMERICA", OLINGER, OLSON\par \par \uc2 ATARI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM BROADWAY MUSICAL, "SHUCKED"\par \par \uc2 WATER CRISIS, MANATEE\par \par \uc2 EARTH DAY, SHOPPING, DEALS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 "SHUCKED", NEWELL, HENSON, KELLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EARS FOR NEW MUSICAL "SHUCKED"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY THEATERGOERS KEEP COMING BACK TO THIS PUN-PACKED PRODUCTION\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the brand-new musical quickly becoming one of Broadway's biggest crowd pleasers. And Will Ganss is back with the farm to fable story. Will, good morning. Yes?\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Good morning, Whit. Yeah, the landscape of Broadway is changing, y'all. "The Phantom of the Opera" has officially ghosted, but there's a new talent of crop stepping into the spotlight. So, shuckle up, y'all.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM BROADWAY SHOW, "SHUCKED"\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "Shucked" the brand-new musical bursting with corn puns, some innuendo, a dazzling dose of dad jokes and audiences are all ears.\par \par \uc2 ALEX NEWELL ("SHUCKED")\par \par \uc2 It's about corn. It's about corn.\par \par \uc2 GREY HENSON ("STORYTELLER 2")\par \par \uc2 We just say it's about corn.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY D KELLEY ("STORYTELLER 1")\par \par \uc2 Literally because it is.\par \par \uc2 GREY HENSON ("STORYTELLER 2")\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I say it's like a sweet simple show that knows what it is and it's about corn.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) More than a decade in the making, "Shucked" is a totally original story not based on a movie. Instead theatergoers gravitating towards the show's corn-centrated advertising efforts. Surreptitiously on light poles across Manhattan. Corny reviews from the likes of Elon Husk, a starch madness social media campaign, all of it buttering up Broadway's newest viral sensation.\par \par \uc2 GREY HENSON ("STORYTELLER 2")\par \par \uc2 People assume it was inspired by the sweet boy who did the corn video on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY D KELLEY ("STORYTELLER 1")\par \par \uc2 Oh, yes. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHILD (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I love corn. Mm, corn.\par \par \uc2 GREY HENSON ("STORYTELLER 2")\par \par \uc2 They assume that the musical was borne out of that. And so, it coming to Broadway and it has nothing to do with TikTok.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How does it feel to yell and bring to y'all something totally fresh to Broadway?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY D KELLEY ("STORYTELLER 1")\par \par \uc2 It's so refreshing. Because, you know, I love a good jukebox musical. But, you know, there has been something missing from Broadway for pretty long time now. And it's an honor to bring something completely new to Broadway.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fans lining up outside the Nederlander Theater, eight shows a week. Some already seeing "Shucked" more than ten times.\par \par \uc2 ALEX NEWELL ("SHUCKED")\par \par \uc2 That's the joy that they're feeling. You know how many times I've watched "The Devil Wears Prada"? Too many times. You gravitate towards things you enjoy seeing.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the show tunes coming from the country music masterminds, Shane McAnally and Brandy Clark. The duo writing Alex Newell a show stopping number earning them a standing O mid-show.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, as an audience member, was like, it's happening. We're doing it. Like, it was thrilling for me. What is that moment like for you every night?\par \par \uc2 ALEX NEWELL ("SHUCKED")\par \par \uc2 I'm exhausted. I just spent three minutes screaming at the audience. So, I'm exhausted. I feel like a super hero in that one moment. My hands are on my hips and I'm looking up into a light and I just see light shining at me and just hear a wall of sound screaming. It's thrilling, it is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is the magic of this musical?\par \par \uc2 GREY HENSON ("STORYTELLER 2")\par \par \uc2 I think the musical represents a sense of humor and a sense of family and community, right? And so, in that, it connects with everyone.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY D KELLEY ("STORYTELLER 1")\par \par \uc2 I also think the magic comes from the fact that it's unexpected. You know, when people come, there's no expectation and they don't know what it is. And then they sit here for two-and-a-half hours and they go through all of the emotions, not expecting to feel feelings on a show about corn.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The jokes come so fast and furiously in "Shucked" that the audience is essentially another cast member according to the actors on stage and everyone wants to know is it really about corn? Yeah, it is.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It literally is. Literally is.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's actually about corn?\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's about corn. A few other things, too.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) My face hurt after that show because I couldn't stop laughing.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rave review for sure. Will, thank you so much. Coming up here, Ryan Seacrest pulling back the curtain on "American Idol" ahead of the live show starting tomorrow. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 A MOMENT LIKE THIS BEHIND THE SCENES TOUR OF NEW "AMERICAN IDOL" SET\b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATION, WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, PARENTING, GRUNDEY\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2 , KYRIACOU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO CORONATION DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FMR COMMUNICATIONS SECRETARY ON HOW \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2  IS GETTING ROYALLY READY\par \par \uc2 EARTH DAY, "OUR AMERICA", OLINGER, OLSON\par \par \uc2 ATARI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM BROADWAY MUSICAL, "SHUCKED"\par \par \uc2 WATER CRISIS, MANATEE\par \par \uc2 EARTH DAY, SHOPPING, DEALS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 "SHUCKED", NEWELL, HENSON, KELLEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EARS FOR NEW MUSICAL "SHUCKED"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY THEATERGOERS KEEP COMING BACK TO THIS PUN-PACKED PRODUCTION\par \par \uc2 "AMERICAN IDOL", SEACREST\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to "GMA." And a moment like this for this season's top 20 set to perform live tomorrow night. And first on "GMA," host Ryan Seacrest is showing off the first set. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A MOMENT LIKE THIS\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RYAN SEACREST GIVES BEHIND THE SCENES TOUR OF NEW "AMERICAN IDOL" SET\par \par \uc2 RYAN SEACREST (HOST "AMERICAN IDOL")\par \par \uc2 Welcome to the new "American Idol" studios. We parked there. But this where it actually happens. Sorry. It's a brand-new stage and set with all the performers will sing for America's vote. Lionel, Katy, Luke's trailers all here. And then, of course, this is where we hangout, decompress, Zen, gather our thoughts before we go on the air live. This studio has a lot of history. This is where they shot "I Love Lucy," also "The Golden Girls," and now "American Idol." And that is going to do it for the behind-the-scenes tour of "Idol." Make sure you tune in. Bye.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Get more "GMA" in your day with "GMA Life" streaming on ABC News Live this morning at 9:00 and 11:00 AM and 1:00 PM Eastern.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here at home now to our other top story this morning, and the Supreme Court decision on the abortion pill. Mifepristone remains approved for use in America, at least for now, while legal challenges continue. ABC's Elizabeth Schulze at the Supreme Court with the decision seen as a relief by abortion right advocates and pharmaceutical companies. Elizabeth, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. Well, this decision means that Mifepristone will stay available to women across the country for now as this legal battle over the medication used in more than half of US abortions continues to play out. So, in a decision 7-2 last night at the Supreme Court, the court put a pause on rulings from lower courts that would have severely restricted access to Mifepristone. Conservative Justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  dissented, indicating that they would have let those restrictions go into effect. Now, the court's decision, as you say, is a big relief for abortion rights advocates, for the pharmaceutical industry, for the Biden administration. They all oppose a ruling by a federal judge in Texas who sided with antiabortion activists saying Mifepristone should have never been approved by the fda more than 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: APPEALS COURT TO HEAR ARGUMENTS IN TEXAS CASE IN MAY\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden saying in a statement last night, "I continue to stand by \uc2 FDA\uc2 's evidence-based approval of Mifepristone. The stakes could not be higher for women across America." So what happens now? Starting May 17th, the merits of that Texas case will be heard in oral arguments at the Conservative Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals. That decision could ultimately end up back here at the Supreme Court. So, bottom line, status quo on the abortion pill as this winds through the courts, but the Supreme Court has now weighed in on abortion access twice in the last year after overturning Roe V Wade. This legal fight is far from over. Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A long legal battle ahead. Thank you so much, Elizabeth Schulze. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 09:00 HALF-HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 557 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's our second hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EVACUATIONS TO BEGIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN ESCALATES\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 More gunfire amid a fragile 72-hour ceasefire as the crisis in Sudan escalates. The Sudanese army saying foreign diplomats will be evacuated in the coming hours.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT'S LATEST DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION PILL ACCESS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Overnight, the Supreme Court's latest decision, preserving access to the abortion pill for now. Reaction this morning as the ruling sets up new battle over abortion almost one year after the court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OHIO MOM OPENS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIKTOK TIP HELPS FIND LOST TODDLER\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Meet the Ohio mom crediting a parenting tip she saw on TikTok helping find her lost toddler lost in a public play area.\par \par \uc2 KRISTA PIPER GRUNDEY (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Little girl. Pink shirt. Minnie Mouse.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 What she's telling "GMA." Plus, the expert safety advice every parent should know.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GETTING ROYALLY READY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO CORONATION\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Countdown to coronation. Exactly two weeks until \uc2 King Charles\uc2  is crowned. This morning, what his former communication secretary is saying about how the monarch is getting royally ready.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ATARI ACQUIRES MORE THAN 100 GAMES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAME CHANGER\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Game changer. \uc2 Atari\uc2  making a play for nostalgia, purchasing the rights over 100 games. What the big move means for fans.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUTTERING UP BROADWAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUDIENCES ALL EARS FOR "SHUCKED"\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 It's the musical buttering up Broadway, and audiences are all ears. "GMA" taking you behind the "Shucked" phenomenon catching up with new crop of talent. And they're saying...\par \par \uc2 GREY HENSON ("STORYTELLER 2")\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY D KELLEY ("STORYTELLER 1")\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 It's Saturday morning. This is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We're hitting all the notes this morning on "Good Morning America."\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Did you catch all the corny puns?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Definitely saw the corny puns in there. You saw the show, too, right?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 It's so good. A lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Great.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Can't wait for that segment.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Excellent. Looking forward to that. Good morning, America. Great to have you with us on this Earth Day. And this morning, we have stories from across the country all about the power of water from one North Carolina community's quest for safe drinking water to so-called manatee miracle happening in Florida.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, a look at some of the high-tech hopes for the future that is all ahead this hour.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 09:30 HALF-HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 270 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATION, WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, PARENTING, GRUNDEY\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2 , KYRIACOU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO CORONATION DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FMR COMMUNICATIONS SECRETARY ON HOW \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2  IS GETTING ROYALLY READY\par \par \uc2 EARTH DAY, "OUR AMERICA", OLINGER, OLSON\par \par \uc2 ATARI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM BROADWAY MUSICAL, "SHUCKED"\par \par \uc2 ALEX NEWELL ("SHUCKED")\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "GMA." And Alex Newel starring in the brand-new musical that's propping up on Broadway. Coming up this half hour, why audiences are all ears for "Shucked." That's ahead.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "SHUCKED" MUSICAL, COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Alex Newell got a standing ovation when I saw that in that performance.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I love how much you see, how many shows you see.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, we get all caught up on our Broadway from Gio. And we appreciate that for sure. More on that ahead.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 VIRAL PARENTING ADVICE HELPS FIND LOST TODDLER OHIO MOM OPENS UP ABOUT TIKTOK TIP INTO ACTION\b0\par\par\uc2 831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATION, WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, PARENTING, GRUNDEY\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. Let's get right to our "GMA Cover Story" in a TikTok tip that turned into a real lifesaver for one Ohio mom.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIRAL PARENTING ADVICE HELPS FIND LOST TODDLER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OHIO MOM OPENS UP ABOUT TIKTOK TIP INTO ACTION\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, she's opening up about what could have been a parent's worst nightmare. ABC Jaclyn Lee is back with all the details. Jaclyn, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, good morning. The mom tells me she really never thought it would happen to her. But she saw the TikTok a year ago and filed it away. Now, she wants other parents to know what helped her find her daughter.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, it's a parent's worst nightmare, taking their eyes off their child for a split second only for that child to run off.\par \par \uc2 KRISTA PIPER GRUNDEY (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 That's when it really sank in, that this was real. She was lost. I don't know where she was or if someone took her.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mom Krista Pfiefer Grandi visited a museum with her 7-year-old son and 3-year-old daughter Lily. It was packed. She says she was talking to another mom, but then she says Lily ran off and didn't return.\par \par \uc2 KRISTA PIPER GRUNDEY (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 And so, at that point, I'm like, okay, she's not out here. I kind of start to panic a little bit.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Krista says she started yelling Lily's name, and then remembered an unlikely tip from a TikTok video.\par \par \uc2 KRISTA PIPER GRUNDEY (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 That's when that lightbulb moment went off about that TikTok that I saw from a year ago about how to yell out the description and that's actually easier to find your child versus just yelling out their name.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, that's what she did and then posted it on her TikTok.\par \par \uc2 KRISTA PIPER GRUNDEY (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 So, I raised my voice and I was like, little girl, pink shirt, Minnie Mouse. And I heard behind me after I passed other moms repeating that. And it was like, thank God, and I kept going.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And do you think that yelling her description really helped find her quicker?\par \par \uc2 KRISTA PIPER GRUNDEY (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 It did, yes. It did help find her very quick just because other parents were then looking for that description. You know, they don't know what Lily looks like.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And that is when another mom came to the rescue.\par \par \uc2 KRISTA PIPER GRUNDEY (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 And that's when a mom kind of grabs me and she's like, hey, little girl, pink shirt, right? And she was just like hiding behind the little wall just playing and having no idea that she was lost.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Krista shared her journey online in the hopes that it helps another parent one day.\par \par \uc2 KRISTA PIPER GRUNDEY (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 So, there's tons of those stories about how people lost their kid or found their kid. There are tips about how to, you know, help not lose your child.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we also spoke with an expert from the \uc2 National Center for Missing and Exploited Children\uc2 . He recommends taking a photo of your child before going to a crowded place. That way, if God forbid you can't find your child, there's an accurate photo of what your child looks like and what they were wearing. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Great advice. Yelling out the description.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Genius.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she was saying other people started repeating it and you can really see how it works.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Uh-huh. Because any parent can totally relate and actually want to help.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep, all right, thanks to Jaclyn for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOOD MORNING AMERICA\b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning America. Breaking news. Sudan's army is claiming they're coordinating with a number of countries to evacuate diplomats. US forces prepare to assist should diplomats at the US Embassy be forced to flee. The new explosions after the 72-hour ceasefire fails in the capital city. The latest this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION PILL BATTLE: AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Available for now. The Supreme Court preserves access to the abortion pill. The vote and dissenting opinions, what happens next, and how it could wind up back at the high court.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Severe weather threat. 23 million Americans along the east coast in the cross hairs, facing damaging winds, hail, and possible tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY BLAST ON BRIDGE: TANKER EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tanker explosion. Clouds of black smoke filling the sky from a massive and deadly blast on a key link in the crucial I-95 corridor. Drivers saved with moments to spare. The state of travel this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALEC BALDWIN BACK ON SET: "RUST RETURNS"\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 "Rust" returns. Alec Baldwin seen back on set, taking extra precautions as filming on the movie resumes the same day charges against him in the fatal shooting of a cinematographer are formally dismissed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AI ISSUES: TOM BRADY CALLS A FOUL\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tom Brady calls a foul, threatening legal action over an AI generated video using his likeness.\par \par \uc2 HOST (MALE)\par \par \uc2 That sounds exactly (inaudible) right.\par \par \uc2 HOST (MALE)\par \par \uc2 That was really close.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, the technology's new waves in the music industry.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAUGHT ON CAMERA: DRAMATIC RESCUE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Dramatic rescue. Three college students trapped deep in a canyon, facing high water and hypothermia. The phone feature that saved them with an airlift to safety.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WAS IT AN INSIDE JOB? GOLD HEIST\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Gold heist. Nearly $20 million in gold and other high-value goods disappearing from a major North America airport. Was it an inside job?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTECTING OUR PLANET: EARTH DAY\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And Earth Day. The celebrations around the world to support the efforts to protect our planet.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWER OF WATER\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 "GMA's" deep dive. The struggles for communities with resources running dry. How the hydro-panels are making water literally out of thin air. Other communities awash in different problems disappearing through climate change.\par \par \uc2 RESIDENT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I didn't think it was going to happen in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus the triumphs for humans and the manatee.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Look at her go. Look at her go.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA, SATURDAY, APRIL 22\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from ABC News in New York, this is "Good Morning America."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. So great to have you with us this morning. Women in the United States still, at least for now, have access to Mifepristone, the drug used in the most common method of abortion. We'll have more on that coming up.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEEP DIVE INTO THE NATION'S WATERWAYS FROM RESIDENTS RELOCATING TO "CLOUD SEEDING" A MANATEE MIRACLE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,232 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATION, WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, PARENTING, GRUNDEY\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2 , KYRIACOU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO CORONATION DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FMR COMMUNICATIONS SECRETARY ON HOW \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2  IS GETTING ROYALLY READY\par \par \uc2 EARTH DAY, "OUR AMERICA", OLINGER, OLSON\par \par \uc2 ATARI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM BROADWAY MUSICAL, "SHUCKED"\par \par \uc2 WATER CRISIS, MANATEE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now to the power of water on this Earth Day. ABC News reporting all week on the impact of worldwide water crisis.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "THE POWER OF WATER"\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And our chief meteorologist and managing editor to ABC News climate unit, that's Ginger Zee, telling stories of waterways across the country taking a deep dive all week long and what happens when communities work together. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEEP DIVE INTO THE NATION'S WATERWAYS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FROM RESIDENTS RELOCATING TO "CLOUD SEEDING" A MANATEE MIRACLE\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Throughout Earth week, "GMA" experiencing the power of water across the country. Our first stop, the Colorado River. All of it threatened by more than two decades in a mega-drought. But through weather modification or cloud seeding, they are trying to give the parched river a bunk.\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We can't create cloud, it had to be an existing storm system.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We just give it a bump.\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Particles that act like magnets for water droplets bonding together until heavy enough to fall to the ground as rain or snow. (Inaudible), whose pilot shows us a cloud seeding demonstration flight, says that cloud seeding makes 3% to 15% more snow pact.\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 That water can be priceless. It can make a difference of adding a couple weeks to a year of irrigation cycle for your crops.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As much as cloud seeding is a boost or help, it's not a solution. The main solution is?\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Conservation.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Colorado River has seen record low flows while too much water is posing a threat to other parts of the Gulf Coast. The mighty Mississippi River running through ten states, terminating in the Mississippi Delta and thousands of wetland islands along the Gulf Coast. Some of the islands are being swallowed up.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BRUNET (FORMER RESIDENT OF ISLE DE JEAN CHARLES)\par \par \uc2 I didn't think it was going to happen in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Chris Brunet's hometown on Isle de Jean Charles routinely cut off by storms, homes mangled by Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Once a thriving town of 400 people at its peak, Isle de Jean Charles behind me used to sit on 22,000 acres of land. Now, it's only 320 acres. That's 98% of their land gone.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The island is shrinking due to a few things. Number one, sea level rise. Also, humans constructed levies upstream. That prevented natural sediment to replenish the islands. And then the natural sinking of coastal wetland.\par \par \uc2 TORBJORN TORNQVIST PHD (VOKES GEOLOGY PROFESSOR TULANE UNIVERSITY)\par \par \uc2 When you take all those things together, you're looking at very, very high rise relative to the land. And this is only the beginning. We're going to see many more communities that are going to disappear.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With a $48.3 million federal grant, Louisiana built a new town just 40 miles north and 12 feet higher above sea level. The land and homes free to all recent residents of Isle de Jean Charles.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BRUNET (FORMER RESIDENT OF ISLE DE JEAN CHARLES)\par \par \uc2 I use the word displaced because that's what I feel what I am. But then also, at the same time, this is like a new journey.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The people who now live at New Isle aren't the only ones adapting. The manatees now a symbol of hope in the Crystal River, they congregate in the winter, which boasts bathtub clear water just off Florida's west coast. In 1993, this critical ecosystem was wiped out by a devastating storm, it was known as the storm of the century.\par \par \uc2 LOCAL OFFICIAL (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It virtually killed all the vegetation. A lot of salt water that came in here and the fresh water vegetation couldn't take it.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It has taken decades but the river has been restored to pristine conditions.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How much is a manatee eating on a daily basis?\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 A manatee can eat about 10% of its body weight a day. So, you got to think, if it weighs 1,200 pounds, that's 120 pounds of grass. They have quite the appetite.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To satisfy that appetite, more than 450,000 clusters of eel grass have been planted underwater by hand, creating more than 300 acres of manatee food. And while Crystal River is a shining example of habitat restoration, but manatees elsewhere are not in great shape, threatened by speeding boats. And along Florida's East Coast in the Indian River Lagoon, excessive pollution from development, leaking septic systems and agricultural runoff have all cause massive algae blooms killing off the sea grass manatees rely on.\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Ready one, two, three.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Groups like Zoo Tampa rescuing and rehabilitating more than 300 of these lovable creatures. I got to join in and release two of them.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look at it go. Look at it go.\par \par \uc2 EXPERT (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Once you fix it, you don't want to let it go backwards. You have to make people care by showing them what it could be and what can you do to help.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A really great reporting there from Ginger, the weather team and our colleagues across ABC News. From more of "The Power of Water," you can scan that QR code on the screen right there. Gio, I know you had an experience with the manatees, too.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, you're not supposed to touch them.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And one manatee just came, a little baby manatee came right up to my nose and then he went down and licked my toe. That was very cute. Very cute. And coming up here, more Earth Day "Deals & Steals." We'll be right back. There's Tory ready to go.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GAME ON FOR ATARI COMPANY BUYS RIGHTS TO "BUBSY" & MORE THAN 100 NOSTALGIC GAMES\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATION, WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, PARENTING, GRUNDEY\par \par \uc2 CORONATION, \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2 , KYRIACOU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO CORONATION DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FMR COMMUNICATIONS SECRETARY ON HOW \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2  IS GETTING ROYALLY READY\par \par \uc2 EARTH DAY, "OUR AMERICA", OLINGER, OLSON\par \par \uc2 ATARI\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not to a game-changer and the major announcement from \uc2 Atari\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAME ON FOR ATARI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANY BUYS RIGHTS TO "BUBSY" & MORE THAN 100 NOSTALGIC GAMES\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Remember Atari back in the day? Pre-Nintendo. This has the company making a play for nostalgic fans. Will Ganss is here will all the details on that. Will, good morning.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. Picture it. The year is 1993. Meat Loaf is on the radio singing about what he'd do for love but he won't do that. And you're busy playing "Bubsy" the bobcat for the fourth hour running. Well, those memories of yesteryear are making a comeback of sorts. \uc2 Atari\uc2 , the company that made its mark developing some of the first home gaming systems, including of course the game "Pong," now betting big on nostalgia.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The brand buying rights to more than 100 games from '80s and '90s, including, yes, "Bubsy" and the car combat game Demolition Racer and the baseball series, Hardball.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Atari\uc2  saying, it plans to both release new adaptations of these past story lines and release these older games but on modern gaming consoles. Gaming like you did in the '80s and '90s but with modern tech, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and I remember when I was doing in 1993. Not going to talk about it here. That's for sure. So, we know some gaming giants have won big recently. Obviously, the "Super Mario Brother's" movie a huge success, also "The Last of Us." But are audiences ready for something like Atari "Pong" movie? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think they might be, but there are no word yet on any movies or TV. There was an attempt at a "Bubsy" animated series in 1996, but it was canceled after one episode. One critic saying, it was terrible from beginning to end. Bubsy's voice is so annoying that it can kill your brain cells.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, hey, nostalgia is in baby. This could be "Bubsy's" big second chance, you guys.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. '93 was a good year.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "Bubsy." Thank you so much. We should call you Bubsy, Will.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Bubsy Ganss.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Anything else.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, still on this Earth Day, "GMA's" deep dive into the power of water. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN SUDAN: DIPLOMAT EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, SAUDI ARABIA, DIPLOMATS, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 SEVERE, WEATHER, STORMS, EAST COAST\par \par \uc2 CONNECTICUT, TANKER, FIRE, RODGERS, LAMONT, DASILVA, STEVENS, EUCALITTO\par \par \uc2 US, UKRAINE, TROOPS, TRAINING, TANKS, ABRAMS, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 "RUST", BALDWIN, CHARGES, MANSLAUGHTER\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, POLICE, RACISM, HOMOPHOBIA, LAWSUIT, BURRIS, KHAN, THORPE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SUSPECT, CUSTODY, NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 PASTOR, HYDRO-PANELS, WATER, WOODBERRY, GRAVES\par \par \uc2 SUSPECT, ROCK, DRIVER, KELLY\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE, IPHONE, FEATURE, WOODS, WATTS\par \par \uc2 HEIST, GOLD, MILLIONS, TORONTO, GARRETT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DRAKE, RHIANNA, AI, LAWSUIT, DAVID\par \par \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SUDAN, EVACUATION, WAR\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, SUPREME COURT, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT PRESERVES ACCESS TO ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING 7-2, THE HIGH COURT ALLOWS DRUG TO REMAIN AVAILABLE FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back to this country now and to the Supreme Court's decision overnight on the abortion pill. Mifepristone remains approve for use in America at least for now while legal challenges continue. ABC's Elizabeth Schulze is live at the Supreme Court this morning with the very latest. Good morning again, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning again, Janai. The Supreme Court decision means the abortion pill mifepristone will stay available to women across the country for now as this legal battle over the medication used in more than half of US abortions continues to play out.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, in a 7-2 decision, last night, the High Court put a pause on rulings by lower courts that would have severely restricted access to mifepristone. Conservative justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  dissented, indicating that they would have let those restrictions go into effect. The court's decision is a big relief for abortion rights advocates, the pharmaceutical industry and the Biden administration. They all oppose a ruling by a federal judge in Texas who sided with antiabortion activists, saying mifepristone should have never been approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  more than 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: APPEALS COURT TO HEAR ARGUMENTS IN TEXAS CASE IN MAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden saying in a statement last night, "I continue to stand by \uc2 FDA\uc2 's evidence-based approval of mifepristone. The stakes could not be higher for women across the country."\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, bottom line, the status quo on the abortion pill remains in effect as this case works through the courts. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Elizabeth, so the Supreme Court put a pause on restrictions but walk us through what happens next in this legal process.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, Janai. So, this case now, goes back to the conservative Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIFEPRISTONE LEGAL BATTLE\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SCHULZE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Starting May 17th, the merits of that Texas case will be heard in oral arguments at that appeals court. A decision could come by the end of the summer, and then it could ultimately be sent back here to the Supreme Court. We have already seen the Supreme Court weigh in twice on abortion after overturning Roe v. Wade last year. And this legal fight is not over yet. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So much to watch for, still coming up. Elizabeth, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 3 MINUTES, LIFE-SAVING PARENTING TIP\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And still coming up in our "GMA Morning Menu," meet the Ohio mom who credits a parenting tip she saw on social media in helping find her lost toddler. What she's telling "GMA." Plus, the expert safety advice every parent should know.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 6 MINUTES, CORONATION COUNTDOWN\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also ahead, countdown to coronation day for \uc2 King Charles\uc2  just two weeks away. And his former communication secretary is live with how the monarch is getting royally ready.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEXT HALF HOUR\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, go green with Earth Day "Deals and Steals." And then it's game on with our buddy Will Ganss. Hey, Will.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 14 MINUTES, GAME ON FOR ATARI\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Gio, it's me. \uc2 Atari\uc2  is buying the rights for over 100 games. What the move means if you're nostalgic for the arcades. Stay tuned. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230530ej4m0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEATHER\b0\par\par\uc2 376 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\